/* AI brain Q&A pack 18: animals in depth */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["tell me about leopards | leopard facts | facts about leopards", "**Leopards** are spotted big cats that live in Africa and parts of Asia. They are amazing climbers and often drag their food up into trees to keep it safe from lions and hyenas. Their spots are called **rosettes** because they look like little roses."],
  ["tell me about jaguars | jaguar facts | facts about jaguars", "The **jaguar** is the biggest cat in the Americas, living mostly in rainforests and wetlands. It has one of the strongest bites of any cat and, unlike most cats, it loves to swim. Jaguars look like leopards, but their rosettes often have small dots inside."],
  ["tell me about snow leopards | snow leopard facts | where do snow leopards live", "**Snow leopards** live high in the cold mountains of Central Asia. Their thick fur, wide furry paws and very long tail help them survive, and they wrap that tail around themselves like a scarf when resting. Unlike lions and tigers, they can't roar."],
  ["tell me about cougars | cougar facts | what is a puma | is a mountain lion the same as a cougar", "A **cougar**, **puma** and **mountain lion** are all the same animal! It lives across the Americas, from Canada down to the Andes. Cougars can't roar, but they can purr, hiss and make loud screams."],
  ["tell me about lynx | lynx facts | what is a lynx", "A **lynx** is a medium-sized wild cat with tufts of black fur on its ears and a short tail. Its huge furry paws work like snowshoes in deep snow. The Canada lynx mostly hunts snowshoe hares."],
  ["tell me about bobcats | bobcat facts | what is a bobcat", "**Bobcats** are North American wild cats about twice the size of a house cat. They get their name from their short, \"bobbed\" tail. They are shy and mostly hunt rabbits and rodents around dawn and dusk."],
  ["tell me about caracals | caracal facts | what is a caracal", "The **caracal** is a wild cat from Africa and Asia with long black ear tufts. It is a super jumper and can leap high into the air to snatch birds as they take off."],
  ["tell me about servals | serval facts | what is a serval", "The **serval** is an African wild cat with the longest legs for its body size of any cat. It uses its big ears to hear rodents moving in tall grass, then pounces on them with a high leap."],
  ["tell me about ocelots | ocelot facts | what is an ocelot", "**Ocelots** are spotted wild cats from Central and South America, about twice the size of a house cat. They are mostly nocturnal, good swimmers and climbers, and each one has its own unique pattern of spots and stripes."],
  ["tell me about hyenas | hyena facts | do hyenas really laugh", "Hyenas make a giggling sound when they're excited or nervous, which is why people call them \"laughing\" hyenas. Spotted hyenas are strong hunters, not just scavengers, and their powerful jaws can crush bones. Surprisingly, hyenas are more closely related to cats and mongooses than to dogs."],
  ["tell me about meerkats | meerkat facts | why do meerkats stand up", "**Meerkats** are small members of the mongoose family that live in groups in the deserts of southern Africa. One meerkat often stands up tall as a lookout and gives an alarm call if it spots a hawk or other danger. They live in underground burrows to stay safe and cool."],
  ["tell me about mongooses | mongoose facts | can a mongoose beat a cobra", "**Mongooses** are quick, small carnivores from Africa and Asia. Some kinds can fight venomous snakes like cobras, thanks to super-fast reflexes, thick fur and a body that is partly resistant to snake venom."],
  ["tell me about warthogs | warthog facts | why do warthogs kneel", "**Warthogs** are wild pigs from Africa with curved tusks and bumps on their faces. They often kneel on their front knees to eat short grass and dig for roots. When chased, they run into burrows backwards so their tusks face the danger."],
  ["tell me about wildebeest | wildebeest facts | what is the great migration", "**Wildebeest**, also called gnus, are large African antelopes. Every year, more than a million of them travel in a giant loop across the Serengeti and Maasai Mara looking for fresh grass, along with zebras and gazelles. This is called the **Great Migration**. Baby wildebeest can stand and run within minutes of being born."],
  ["tell me about gazelles | gazelle facts | why do gazelles jump straight up", "**Gazelles** are fast, graceful antelopes found in Africa and Asia. When they spot a predator, some bounce straight up with stiff legs, called **pronking** or **stotting**, which may show the predator they are fit and not worth chasing."],
  ["tell me about impalas | impala facts | how high can an impala jump", "**Impalas** are African antelopes famous for their leaps. They can jump about 3 meters (10 feet) high and around 10 meters (33 feet) far, often zig-zagging to confuse predators."],
  ["tell me about okapis | okapi facts | what is an okapi", "The **okapi** looks like a mix of a zebra and a horse, but its closest relative is actually the giraffe! It lives in the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and has a long tongue it can use to clean its own ears."],
  ["tell me about aardvarks | aardvark facts | what is an aardvark", "The **aardvark** is an African mammal whose name means \"earth pig\" in Afrikaans. It digs into termite mounds with strong claws and slurps up ants and termites with its long, sticky tongue. Aardvarks are nocturnal and fantastic diggers."],
  ["tell me about pangolins | pangolin facts | what is a pangolin", "**Pangolins** are the only mammals covered in hard scales, which are made of keratin like your fingernails. When scared, they roll into a tight ball. They eat ants and termites and are sadly among the most illegally traded mammals, so many are endangered."],
  ["tell me about armadillos | armadillo facts | can armadillos roll into a ball", "**Armadillo** means \"little armored one\" in Spanish, thanks to its bony shell. Only the three-banded armadillo can roll up into a complete ball. Nine-banded armadillos usually give birth to four identical babies at once!"],
  ["tell me about anteaters | anteater facts | how long is an anteater tongue", "The **giant anteater** has no teeth but a tongue about 60 cm (2 feet) long. It can flick its tongue in and out over 150 times a minute to eat thousands of ants and termites a day. It lives in Central and South America."],
  ["tell me about tapirs | tapir facts | what is a tapir", "**Tapirs** are plant-eating animals with a short, bendy snout used to grab leaves. They live in Central and South America and Southeast Asia, and their closest relatives are horses and rhinos. Baby tapirs have stripes and spots that look a bit like a watermelon."],
  ["tell me about capybaras | capybara facts | are capybaras friendly", "The **capybara** is the world's biggest rodent and lives in South America. It loves water, has slightly webbed feet and can stay underwater for several minutes. Capybaras are so calm that birds and other animals often sit on them."],
  ["tell me about porcupines | porcupine facts | can porcupines shoot their quills", "Porcupines can't shoot their quills, but the quills come loose very easily when touched. Each quill has tiny backward barbs that make it hard to pull out. A North American porcupine can have around 30,000 quills!"],
  ["tell me about chinchillas | chinchilla facts | why do chinchillas take dust baths", "**Chinchillas** come from the Andes mountains of South America and have some of the softest, thickest fur of any land animal. Their fur is so dense that water doesn't dry well in it, so they roll in fine dust to keep clean instead of taking water baths."],
  ["tell me about ferrets | ferret facts | are ferrets good pets", "**Ferrets** are playful pets related to weasels. They sleep around 14 to 18 hours a day and do a bouncy, excited hop called the \"weasel war dance.\" They need lots of playtime, a safe space and owners who are ready for a mischievous pet."],
  ["tell me about weasels | weasel facts | what is a weasel", "**Weasels** are small, long, thin hunters that can chase mice right into their burrows. The least weasel is the smallest meat-eating mammal in the world. In snowy places, some weasels turn white for winter."],
  ["tell me about stoats | stoat facts | what is an ermine", "A **stoat** is a small weasel-like hunter. In cold places its coat turns white in winter except for the black tip of its tail, and then it is called an **ermine**. Stoats sometimes do a crazy hopping dance that may confuse rabbits."],
  ["tell me about badgers | badger facts | where do badgers live", "**Badgers** are strong, short-legged diggers with striped faces. European badgers live in big tunnel systems called **setts** that can be used by families for many years. They come out at night to eat worms, insects, roots and fruit."],
  ["tell me about honey badgers | honey badger facts | why are honey badgers so tough", "**Honey badgers** live in Africa and Asia and are famous for being fearless. Their skin is thick and loose, so they can twist around and bite back if grabbed. They eat almost anything, from insects and snakes to bee larvae and honey."],
  ["tell me about wolverines | wolverine facts | what is a wolverine", "The **wolverine** is the largest land-dwelling member of the weasel family and lives in cold northern forests and mountains. It is very strong for its size, travels huge distances, and can find food buried deep under the snow."],
  ["tell me about skunks | skunk facts | how far can a skunk spray", "**Skunks** can spray a stinky liquid up to about 3 meters (10 feet) to defend themselves. Before spraying they give warnings, like stamping their feet, hissing or raising their tail. Some spotted skunks even do a handstand first!"],
  ["tell me about opossums | opossum facts | why do possums play dead", "The **Virginia opossum** is the only marsupial in North America, and mothers carry their babies in a pouch and later on their backs. When very frightened, an opossum may faint and \"play dead\" without choosing to, sometimes for hours. They are also very resistant to many snake venoms."],
  ["tell me about red pandas | red panda facts | is a red panda a panda", "**Red pandas** are not closely related to giant pandas; they're in a family all of their own. They live in the mountain forests of the Himalayas and mostly eat bamboo. Their bushy ringed tail helps them balance and keeps them warm when wrapped around their body."],
  ["tell me about lemurs | lemur facts | where do lemurs live", "**Lemurs** are primates that live in the wild only on the island of Madagascar. There are about 100 kinds! Ring-tailed lemurs even have \"stink fights,\" where males wave their smelly tails at each other."],
  ["tell me about orangutans | orangutan facts | what does orangutan mean", "**Orangutan** means \"person of the forest\" in Malay. These great apes with orange-red hair live in the rainforests of Borneo and Sumatra and spend most of their lives in trees. Every night they build a fresh nest of leaves and branches to sleep in."],
  ["tell me about gibbons | gibbon facts | what is a gibbon", "**Gibbons** are small apes from Southeast Asia that swing through trees arm over arm, called **brachiation**. They are the fastest tree-travelers among the primates. Gibbon pairs sing loud songs together in the morning."],
  ["tell me about baboons | baboon facts | what is a baboon", "**Baboons** are large monkeys from Africa and Arabia with dog-like snouts. They live in big groups called troops, spend lots of time on the ground and eat almost anything. They communicate with many sounds and facial expressions."],
  ["tell me about mandrills | mandrill facts | what monkey has a colorful face", "The **mandrill** is the world's largest monkey and has a bright red and blue face. It lives in the rainforests of Central Africa. The brightest colors belong to the most important males."],
  ["tell me about bonobos | bonobo facts | what is the difference between a bonobo and a chimp", "**Bonobos** look like slimmer chimpanzees and, together with chimps, are our closest living relatives. They live only in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Bonobo groups are usually led by females and tend to be more peaceful than chimp groups."],
  ["tell me about tarsiers | tarsier facts | what animal has eyes bigger than its brain", "**Tarsiers** are tiny primates from Southeast Asia. Each of their eyes is about as big as their whole brain! They can't move their eyes, so they turn their heads almost all the way around to look behind them."],
  ["tell me about slow lorises | slow loris facts | is the slow loris venomous", "The **slow loris** is a small, big-eyed primate from Southeast Asia. It is one of the very few venomous mammals: it mixes a substance from a gland on its arm with its saliva. They move slowly and quietly through trees at night."],
  ["tell me about moose | moose facts | how big is a moose", "The **moose** is the largest kind of deer. A big male can stand over 2 meters (6.5 feet) tall at the shoulder, and his antlers can be about 1.8 meters (6 feet) wide. Moose are excellent swimmers and even dive to eat water plants."],
  ["tell me about elk | elk facts | why do elk bugle", "**Elk**, also called wapiti, are large deer from North America and Asia. In autumn, males make a loud, high whistling call called a **bugle** to attract females and challenge rivals. Each year males grow huge new antlers, then shed them in late winter."],
  ["tell me about reindeer | reindeer facts | are reindeer and caribou the same", "Yes! **Reindeer** and **caribou** are the same species. They live in the Arctic, and both males and females grow antlers. Their eyes even change color from gold in summer to blue in winter to help them see in the dark months."],
  ["tell me about bison | bison facts | what is the difference between bison and buffalo", "**Bison** are the largest land animals in North America and are often called buffalo, though true buffalo live in Africa and Asia. Tens of millions once roamed the plains, but by the late 1800s only a few hundred were left. Thanks to protection, there are now hundreds of thousands again."],
  ["tell me about yaks | yak facts | what is a yak", "**Yaks** are shaggy cattle that live high in the Himalayas and on the Tibetan Plateau. Their long, thick coat keeps them warm in freezing weather. People use them to carry loads and for milk, wool and butter."],
  ["tell me about water buffalo | water buffalo facts", "**Water buffalo** are large cattle that love wallowing in water and mud. People in Asia have used them for thousands of years to plow rice fields. Their milk is used to make real mozzarella cheese."],
  ["tell me about llamas | llama facts | what is the difference between a llama and an alpaca", "**Llamas** and **alpacas** both come from South America and are relatives of camels. Llamas are bigger with long banana-shaped ears and are used to carry loads or guard sheep. Alpacas are smaller with short ears and are raised for their very soft wool."],
  ["tell me about alpacas | alpaca facts | do alpacas spit", "**Alpacas** are fluffy animals from the Andes raised for their soft, warm fiber. They do sometimes spit, but mostly at each other when arguing over food, not at people. They hum to communicate and share a group toilet spot."],
  ["tell me about donkeys | donkey facts | why do donkeys bray", "**Donkeys** are strong, careful animals that descend from the African wild ass. Their loud \"hee-haw\" bray can carry a long way, which helped wild donkeys call to each other across deserts. They have great memories and can be very loyal."],
  ["what is a mule | what is a mule animal | what is a hinny", "A **mule** is the baby of a male donkey and a female horse. Mules are strong, sure-footed and hard-working, but they almost never have babies of their own. A **hinny** is the opposite mix: a male horse and a female donkey."],
  ["tell me about mountain goats | mountain goat facts | how do mountain goats climb so well", "**Mountain goats** live on steep, rocky cliffs in North America. Their split hooves have rough, grippy pads on the bottom that work like climbing shoes. Even babies can climb and jump within hours of being born."],
  ["tell me about ibex | ibex facts | why do goats climb dams", "**Ibex** are wild mountain goats with long curved horns. In Italy, Alpine ibex have been seen climbing nearly vertical dam walls to lick salt and minerals from the stone. Their hooves grip rock amazingly well."],
  ["tell me about musk oxen | musk ox facts | what is a musk ox", "**Musk oxen** are shaggy Arctic animals that survived the Ice Age. Under their long outer hair is a super-soft, warm wool called **qiviut**. When wolves come near, adults form a circle with their horns facing out and the young safe inside."],
  ["tell me about arctic foxes | arctic fox facts | how do arctic foxes survive the cold", "**Arctic foxes** have thick fur, furry paw pads and short ears and legs that help them hold on to heat. Their coat is white in winter and brown or gray in summer, so they blend in all year. They can stay comfortable at temperatures far below freezing."],
  ["tell me about arctic hares | arctic hare facts", "**Arctic hares** live in the far north and have thick white fur in winter. They often dig shelters in the snow and huddle in groups to stay warm. When escaping danger they can hop on their back legs at about 60 km/h (40 mph)."],
  ["tell me about walruses | walrus facts | what do walruses use their tusks for", "**Walruses** are big Arctic marine mammals with long tusks, which both males and females have. They use their tusks to pull themselves out onto the ice and to show who's boss. Their sensitive whiskers help them find clams on the dark sea floor."],
  ["tell me about seals | seal facts | what is the difference between a seal and a sea lion", "**Seals** have no ear flaps and wiggle on their bellies on land. **Sea lions** have small ear flaps, can turn their back flippers forward to walk, and are usually louder. Both are great swimmers that eat fish and squid."],
  ["tell me about sea lions | sea lion facts | why do sea lions bark", "**Sea lions** are playful marine mammals that bark loudly to talk to each other and defend their space. They can \"walk\" on land using all four flippers. In the water, they are fast, twisty swimmers."],
  ["tell me about manatees | manatee facts | why are manatees called sea cows", "**Manatees** are slow, gentle marine mammals often called sea cows because they graze on sea grass and water plants. They live in warm waters and their closest living relatives on land are elephants! They come up to breathe air every few minutes."],
  ["tell me about dugongs | dugong facts | what is a dugong", "The **dugong** is a cousin of the manatee that lives in the warm coastal waters of the Indian and Pacific Oceans. It has a tail shaped like a dolphin's and eats sea grass. Sailors' stories of mermaids may have come from seeing dugongs and manatees."],
  ["tell me about narwhals | narwhal facts | is a narwhal tusk a horn", "The narwhal's \"horn\" is actually a long tooth that grows through its upper lip, usually in males, and can reach about 3 meters (10 feet). Narwhals live in the Arctic and are sometimes called the **unicorns of the sea**."],
  ["tell me about beluga whales | beluga facts | why are belugas white", "**Belugas** are white whales that live in cold Arctic waters, where their color helps them blend in with ice. They make so many chirps and whistles that they're nicknamed **sea canaries**. Unlike most whales, they can turn their necks."],
  ["tell me about humpback whales | humpback whale facts | why do humpback whales sing", "**Humpback whales** are famous for their long, beautiful songs, which are sung by males and change slowly over time. They leap out of the water in huge splashes called breaches. Groups sometimes blow rings of bubbles to trap fish, called **bubble-net feeding**."],
  ["tell me about sperm whales | sperm whale facts | how deep can a sperm whale dive", "The **sperm whale** is the largest animal with teeth and has the biggest brain of any animal. It can dive deeper than 2,000 meters (6,500 feet) to hunt squid in the dark. Sperm whales also communicate with patterns of clicks."],
  ["what is the difference between a dolphin and a porpoise | tell me about porpoises | porpoise facts", "**Porpoises** are usually smaller than dolphins, with a rounder face and no long beak. Their teeth are flat and spade-shaped, while dolphins have cone-shaped teeth. Porpoises also tend to be shyer and have triangle-shaped back fins."],
  ["tell me about wombats | wombat facts | why is wombat poop square", "**Wombats** are sturdy Australian marsupials that dig burrows. They are famous for making cube-shaped poop, which helps it stay put when they leave it on rocks and logs to mark their area. A mother's pouch faces backward so dirt doesn't get in while she digs."],
  ["tell me about tasmanian devils | tasmanian devil facts | are tasmanian devils real", "Yes! **Tasmanian devils** are real marsupials that live on the Australian island of Tasmania. They are the largest meat-eating marsupials alive today, have very strong jaws and make spooky screeches at night. Conservationists are working hard to protect them from a serious disease."],
  ["tell me about quokkas | quokka facts | why do quokkas smile", "The **quokka** is a small marsupial from Western Australia, mostly found on Rottnest Island. The shape of its mouth makes it look like it's smiling, so it's often called the happiest animal in the world. Visitors are asked not to touch or feed them."],
  ["tell me about wallabies | wallaby facts | what is the difference between a wallaby and a kangaroo", "**Wallabies** are marsupials from the same family as kangaroos, just usually smaller. Like kangaroos, they hop on strong back legs and carry babies, called joeys, in a pouch. There are many kinds, including rock-wallabies that bounce around cliffs."],
  ["tell me about sugar gliders | sugar glider facts | can sugar gliders fly", "**Sugar gliders** are tiny marsupials from Australia and New Guinea. They can't truly fly, but they spread a stretchy skin flap between their legs and glide from tree to tree, sometimes around 50 meters. They love sweet tree sap and nectar."],
  ["tell me about echidnas | echidna facts | what is an echidna", "**Echidnas** are spiny mammals from Australia and New Guinea that lay eggs, just like the platypus. They use their long snout and sticky tongue to eat ants and termites. A baby echidna is called a **puggle**!"],
  ["tell me about dingoes | dingo facts | what is a dingo", "The **dingo** is Australia's wild dog. Dingoes arrived in Australia thousands of years ago and are now an important top predator there. They rarely bark and instead howl, yelp and whine."],
  ["tell me about numbats | numbat facts | what is a numbat", "The **numbat** is a striped marsupial from Western Australia that eats almost nothing but termites, up to about 20,000 a day! Unlike most marsupials, it is active during the day, when termites are near the surface."],
  ["tell me about bilbies | bilby facts | what is the easter bilby", "The **bilby** is a small Australian marsupial with long rabbit-like ears and a long nose. Because rabbits harm Australia's wildlife, many Australians celebrate with chocolate **Easter Bilbies** instead of bunnies to help raise money for bilbies."],
  ["tell me about fennec foxes | fennec fox facts | why do fennec foxes have big ears", "The **fennec fox** is the smallest fox in the world and lives in the Sahara Desert. Its giant ears help it hear insects underground and release body heat to stay cool. Furry feet protect it from the hot sand."],
  ["tell me about coyotes | coyote facts | why do coyotes howl", "**Coyotes** are clever wild dogs found across North and Central America, even in big cities. They howl and yip to tell other coyotes where they are and to keep their family together. They eat almost anything, from mice to fruit."],
  ["tell me about jackals | jackal facts | what is a jackal", "**Jackals** are medium-sized wild dogs from Africa, Asia and southeastern Europe. Pairs often stay together for life and work as a team to hunt and raise pups. They eat small animals, fruit and leftovers."],
  ["tell me about african wild dogs | african wild dog facts | what is a painted wolf", "**African wild dogs**, also called painted wolves, have patchy coats where no two are the same. They hunt in packs and are among the most successful hunters in Africa. Pack members share food and even help feed the pups."],
  ["tell me about naked mole rats | naked mole rat facts | what is a naked mole rat", "**Naked mole rats** are wrinkly, nearly hairless rodents that live in underground colonies in East Africa. Like bees, each colony has one queen who has all the babies. They can live over 30 years, which is amazing for a rodent!"],
  ["tell me about moles | mole facts | can moles see", "**Moles** spend almost their whole lives digging tunnels underground with their huge shovel-shaped front paws. Their eyes are tiny and they see very little, so they rely on touch and smell. The star-nosed mole has 22 fleshy tentacles on its nose to feel for food."],
  ["tell me about shrews | shrew facts | is a shrew a mouse", "**Shrews** look like mice but are not rodents; they are more closely related to moles and hedgehogs. Their bodies burn energy so fast that they must eat almost constantly. Some shrews even have venomous saliva to stun insects."],
  ["tell me about flying squirrels | flying squirrel facts | can flying squirrels really fly", "**Flying squirrels** don't actually fly; they glide using flaps of skin stretched between their front and back legs. They can steer with their tails and glide long distances between trees. Scientists even discovered that their fur glows pink under ultraviolet light."],
  ["tell me about prairie dogs | prairie dog facts | are prairie dogs dogs", "**Prairie dogs** aren't dogs; they're rodents in the squirrel family. They live in big underground \"towns\" in North America. Their warning calls are so detailed they can signal the kind of predator coming, and maybe even its size and color."],
  ["tell me about groundhogs | groundhog facts | what is groundhog day", "**Groundhogs**, also called woodchucks, are big ground squirrels that hibernate through winter. On **Groundhog Day**, February 2, a tradition says if a groundhog sees its shadow there will be six more weeks of winter. It's just a fun tradition, not a real weather forecast!"],
  ["tell me about chipmunks | chipmunk facts | how much food can a chipmunk carry", "**Chipmunks** are small striped squirrels. They stuff food into stretchy cheek pouches and carry it back to their burrows to store for winter. Their cheeks can stretch to about three times the size of their head!"],
  ["tell me about lemmings | lemming facts | do lemmings jump off cliffs", "No, **lemmings** don't jump off cliffs on purpose. That myth was spread by an old nature film that staged the scene. Lemmings are small Arctic rodents whose numbers rise and fall in big cycles, and they sometimes migrate in large groups."],
  ["tell me about hyraxes | hyrax facts | what animal is related to the elephant", "The **hyrax** looks like a big guinea pig, but it's actually one of the closest living relatives of elephants, along with manatees and dugongs! Hyraxes live in Africa and the Middle East and love sunbathing on rocks."],
  ["tell me about dik diks | dik dik facts | what is a dik dik", "The **dik-dik** is a tiny African antelope only about 30 to 40 cm (12 to 16 inches) tall at the shoulder. It is named after the alarm call it makes when scared. Dik-dik pairs usually stay together for life."],
  ["tell me about pronghorns | pronghorn facts | what is the fastest animal in north america", "The **pronghorn** is the fastest land animal in North America and can run about 88 km/h (55 mph). Even more amazing, it can keep up high speeds for a long distance, much longer than a cheetah. It looks like an antelope but is in a family all its own."],
  ["tell me about kinkajous | kinkajou facts | what is a kinkajou", "The **kinkajou**, sometimes called a honey bear, is a rainforest mammal related to raccoons. It uses its gripping tail to hang from branches and its long tongue to lick nectar and fruit. It's active at night."],
  ["tell me about coatis | coati facts | what is a coati", "**Coatis** are raccoon relatives from the Americas with long, ringed tails held straight up and long, bendy snouts for sniffing out food. Females and young live in noisy groups called bands."],
  ["tell me about jerboas | jerboa facts | what is a jerboa", "**Jerboas** are tiny desert rodents with long back legs that let them hop like little kangaroos. Some have huge ears, too. They come out at night when the desert is cool."],
  ["tell me about kangaroo rats | kangaroo rat facts | can kangaroo rats live without water", "**Kangaroo rats** are North American desert rodents that hop on big back legs. They can survive without ever drinking water, getting the water they need from the seeds they eat. Their kidneys are great at saving water."],
  ["tell me about sand cats | sand cat facts | what is a sand cat", "The **sand cat** is a small wild cat that lives in deserts of Africa and Asia. Thick fur on its paw pads protects it from hot sand, and it gets most of its water from its food. It looks like a cute house kitten but is fully wild."],
  ["tell me about oryx | oryx facts | how do oryx survive in the desert", "**Oryx** are desert antelopes with long, straight horns. To save water, they let their body temperature rise during the hot day instead of sweating, then cool off at night. They can go a long time without drinking."],
  ["tell me about kangaroos boxing | why do kangaroos box | do kangaroos fight", "Male kangaroos sometimes \"box\" to show who is strongest. They grab each other with their arms and kick with their powerful back legs while leaning on their tail. It's usually a contest, not a fight to the end."],
  ["tell me about ostriches | ostrich facts | do ostriches bury their heads in the sand", "No, ostriches don't bury their heads in the sand; that's a myth. They often lower their heads to turn their eggs in the nest, which might look like that from far away. Ostriches can't fly, but they can run about 70 km/h (43 mph) and lay the biggest eggs of any living bird."],
  ["tell me about emus | emu facts | what is an emu", "The **emu** is a big, flightless bird from Australia and the second-tallest bird in the world, after the ostrich. Emus can run about 50 km/h (30 mph). The dad sits on the eggs for around eight weeks and then looks after the chicks."],
  ["tell me about cassowaries | cassowary facts | are cassowaries dangerous", "**Cassowaries** are large flightless birds from the rainforests of northern Australia and New Guinea. They have a tall helmet called a **casque** and a dagger-like claw on each foot, so they can be dangerous if bothered. They help the rainforest by spreading seeds in their droppings."],
  ["tell me about kiwi birds | kiwi bird facts | can kiwis fly", "**Kiwis** are small, flightless birds from New Zealand and are its national symbol. They are the only birds with nostrils at the tip of their beaks, which they use to sniff out worms. A kiwi egg is huge, around one-fifth of the mother's weight!"],
  ["tell me about toucans | toucan facts | why do toucans have big beaks", "**Toucans** live in Central and South American rainforests. Their giant colorful beaks are surprisingly light because they're made of keratin with a honeycomb-like inside. The beak helps them reach fruit and also lets off extra body heat to keep them cool."],
  ["tell me about puffins | puffin facts | how many fish can a puffin hold", "**Puffins** are seabirds with bright orange beaks in the breeding season, which is why they're called **clowns of the sea**. They dive underwater using their wings and can carry many small fish crosswise in their beaks at once, sometimes more than 10."],
  ["tell me about pelicans | pelican facts | how much can a pelican's pouch hold", "A **pelican's** stretchy throat pouch can hold about 11 liters (3 gallons) of water! The pelican scoops up fish and water, then drains the water out before swallowing. Brown pelicans dive headfirst into the sea from the air to catch fish."],
  ["tell me about albatrosses | albatross facts | which bird has the biggest wingspan", "The **wandering albatross** has the biggest wingspan of any living bird, up to about 3.5 meters (11 feet). Albatrosses can glide over the ocean for hours without flapping their wings. Many pairs stay together for life."],
  ["tell me about seagulls | seagull facts | are seagulls smart", "Scientists just call them **gulls**, since many live inland too. Gulls are clever: some drop shellfish onto rocks to crack them open and some stamp their feet to trick worms into coming up. They can drink salt water and get rid of the extra salt through glands above their eyes."],
  ["tell me about crows | crow facts | how smart are crows", "**Crows** are among the smartest birds. They can solve puzzles, use and even make tools, and recognize individual human faces, remembering people who were kind or unkind to them. They also live in family groups that work together."],
  ["tell me about ravens | raven facts | what is the difference between a crow and a raven", "**Ravens** are bigger than crows and have a wedge-shaped tail, while a crow's tail is fan-shaped. Ravens make deep croaks instead of caws. They are very smart and playful, and have been seen sliding down snowy roofs just for fun."],
  ["tell me about magpies | magpie facts | are magpies smart", "**Magpies** are clever, black-and-white birds in the crow family. Eurasian magpies are among the few animals that seem to recognize themselves in a mirror. The idea that they always steal shiny things is mostly a myth."],
  ["tell me about blue jays | blue jay facts | are blue jays actually blue", "Blue jay feathers have no blue pigment! The blue color comes from the way light bounces off tiny structures inside the feathers. Blue jays are noisy, smart birds that can even copy the calls of hawks."],
  ["tell me about cardinals | cardinal facts | why are cardinals red", "Male **northern cardinals** are bright red, while females are mostly brownish with red tints. Their red color comes from pigments in the food they eat, like berries and seeds. Both males and females sing, which is unusual among songbirds."],
  ["tell me about robins | robin facts | what color are robin eggs", "American robins lay beautiful light blue eggs, which is why that color is called \"robin's egg blue.\" The **American robin** is a type of thrush and is a different bird from the smaller **European robin**. Robins listen and watch carefully for worms on lawns."],
  ["tell me about sparrows | sparrow facts", "**Sparrows** are small, brown, seed-eating birds. The house sparrow is one of the most widespread birds on Earth because it lives close to people almost everywhere. Sparrows often take dust baths to keep their feathers clean."],
  ["tell me about woodpeckers | woodpecker facts | why don't woodpeckers get headaches", "**Woodpeckers** can peck around 20 times a second! Special features of their skull, beak and neck help protect their brains from all that hammering. Their long tongue wraps around the back of the skull when it's pulled in."],
  ["tell me about kingfishers | kingfisher facts | how did a bird inspire a bullet train", "**Kingfishers** dive into water to catch fish with barely a splash. Engineers in Japan copied the shape of the kingfisher's beak to redesign the nose of a bullet train, making it quieter and more efficient."],
  ["tell me about herons | heron facts | how do herons catch fish", "**Herons** are tall wading birds with long legs and sharp beaks. They stand very still in the water, then strike lightning fast to grab fish. They fly with their necks folded into an S shape."],
  ["tell me about storks | stork facts | do storks deliver babies", "No, storks don't deliver babies; that's an old European story! **White storks** often build huge nests on rooftops and chimneys in Europe and migrate to Africa for winter. Since they have no strong voice, they clatter their beaks to communicate."],
  ["tell me about cranes | crane bird facts | why do cranes dance", "**Cranes** are tall birds famous for their dances, where they bow, leap and flap their wings, often to bond with their partners. Unlike herons, they fly with their necks stretched out straight. The whooping crane is one of the rarest birds in North America."],
  ["tell me about swans | swan facts | what is a baby swan called", "A baby swan is called a **cygnet**. Swans are large, graceful waterbirds, and many pairs stay together for years. Most swans are white, but black swans live in Australia."],
  ["tell me about geese | goose facts | why do geese honk", "**Geese** honk to keep in touch with their flock, especially while flying. A male goose is a gander and a baby is a gosling. Geese are very protective, and some farmers even use them as guard animals!"],
  ["tell me about turkeys | turkey facts | can turkeys fly", "Wild turkeys can fly short distances, fast, and they sleep up in trees at night. Farm turkeys are usually too heavy to fly. Male turkeys gobble and have a dangly bit of skin on their beak called a **snood**."],
  ["tell me about pigeons | pigeon facts | how do homing pigeons find their way home", "**Homing pigeons** can find their way home from hundreds of kilometers away, probably using the sun, Earth's magnetic field, smells and landmarks. People once used them to carry messages, even during wars. City pigeons descend from wild rock doves."],
  ["tell me about doves | dove facts | what is the difference between a dove and a pigeon", "**Doves** and **pigeons** are in the same bird family, and there's no strict scientific difference; smaller ones are usually called doves. Both parents make a special \"crop milk\" to feed their chicks."],
  ["tell me about falcons | falcon facts | what is a falcon", "**Falcons** are fast birds of prey with long, pointed wings. They have a special notch in their beaks for catching prey. Falconry, training falcons to hunt with people, has been practiced for thousands of years."],
  ["tell me about hawks | hawk facts | what sound does a hawk make", "**Hawks** are birds of prey with sharp talons and excellent eyesight. The red-tailed hawk's scream is so dramatic that movies often use it for eagles, whose real calls sound more like squeaky chirps!"],
  ["tell me about vultures | vulture facts | why do vultures have bald heads", "**Vultures** are nature's cleanup crew, eating animals that have already died. Their bald heads stay clean while they eat, and their super-strong stomach acid kills germs that would make other animals sick. Without vultures, diseases would spread more easily."],
  ["tell me about condors | condor facts | how big is a condor", "The **Andean condor** is one of the largest flying birds in the world, with a wingspan of about 3 meters (10 feet). The **California condor** almost went extinct in the 1980s, but a breeding program saved it, and now hundreds fly free."],
  ["tell me about ospreys | osprey facts | how do ospreys catch fish", "**Ospreys** are fish-eating birds of prey found on every continent except Antarctica. They dive feet-first into water, and they can turn one toe backwards to grip slippery fish. They carry fish head-first to make flying easier."],
  ["tell me about kookaburras | kookaburra facts | why do kookaburras laugh", "**Kookaburras** are big Australian kingfishers famous for their loud call that sounds like laughing. Families \"laugh\" together, especially at dawn and dusk, to mark their territory."],
  ["tell me about lyrebirds | lyrebird facts | what bird can copy any sound", "The **lyrebird** of Australia is one of the best mimics on Earth. It can copy other birds and even human-made sounds like camera shutters and car alarms. Males show off with a tail shaped like an old musical instrument called a lyre."],
  ["tell me about birds of paradise | bird of paradise facts", "**Birds-of-paradise** live mostly in New Guinea and nearby. Males have amazing colorful feathers and perform wild dances to impress females, sometimes changing their whole body shape."],
  ["tell me about roadrunners | roadrunner facts | how fast is a roadrunner", "**Roadrunners** are desert birds in the cuckoo family that can run around 30 km/h (20 mph). They prefer running to flying and are fast enough to catch lizards and even small rattlesnakes."],
  ["tell me about secretary birds | secretary bird facts", "The **secretary bird** is a tall African bird of prey with long legs. Instead of grabbing prey from the air, it walks through grasslands and stomps on snakes and lizards with powerful kicks."],
  ["tell me about shoebills | shoebill facts | what is a shoebill", "The **shoebill** is a tall African swamp bird with a giant beak shaped like a wooden shoe. It can stand still for a long time waiting for fish, then lunge forward to grab them. Shoebills also clatter their bills loudly."],
  ["tell me about hornbills | hornbill facts", "**Hornbills** are birds of Africa and Asia with large curved beaks, often topped by a helmet called a **casque**. Many females seal themselves inside a tree hole while nesting, and the male passes food through a small slit."],
  ["tell me about swallows | swallow facts", "**Swallows** are graceful birds that catch insects while flying. Barn swallows have a forked tail and travel thousands of kilometers each year to migrate. They build cup-shaped mud nests under roofs and bridges."],
  ["tell me about swifts | swift facts | which bird stays in the air the longest", "**Common swifts** spend almost their entire lives in the air. They eat, drink and even sleep while flying, and some stay in the air for about 10 months without landing!"],
  ["tell me about arctic terns | arctic tern facts | which bird has the longest migration", "The **Arctic tern** has the longest migration of any animal. It flies from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back every year, about 70,000 km (44,000 miles) or more. Because of this, it sees more daylight than any other animal."],
  ["tell me about starlings | starling facts | what is a murmuration", "A **murmuration** is when thousands of starlings fly together in swirling, changing shapes in the sky. Each bird keeps track of its closest neighbors, so the whole flock moves like one. Starlings are also good at copying sounds."],
  ["tell me about canaries | canary facts | why were canaries used in coal mines", "**Canaries** are small songbirds originally from islands in the Atlantic Ocean. Miners once carried canaries underground because the birds got sick from dangerous gases before people did, giving the miners an early warning. Today, electronic detectors do that job."],
  ["tell me about mockingbirds | mockingbird facts", "**Northern mockingbirds** can copy the songs of many other birds and even sounds like car alarms. A single mockingbird may know hundreds of different songs. They're known for singing at night, too."],
  ["tell me about bowerbirds | bowerbird facts", "Male **bowerbirds** of Australia and New Guinea build fancy structures called **bowers** to impress females. The satin bowerbird decorates his bower with blue objects like flowers, berries and even bottle caps."],
  ["tell me about cuckoos | cuckoo bird facts | why do cuckoos lay eggs in other nests", "Some **cuckoos** lay their eggs in other birds' nests, so the other birds raise the cuckoo chick. This is called **brood parasitism**. The cuckoo eggs often look a lot like the host bird's eggs."],
  ["tell me about weaver birds | weaver bird facts", "**Weaver birds** build amazing nests by weaving strips of grass and even tying knots with their beaks. Some kinds build giant shared nests that hold many families."],
  ["tell me about the dodo | what was a dodo | why did the dodo go extinct", "The **dodo** was a large flightless bird from the island of Mauritius, related to pigeons. After sailors arrived in the late 1500s, people hunted it and brought animals like rats and pigs that ate its eggs. It was gone by the late 1600s."],
  ["what was the passenger pigeon | tell me about passenger pigeons", "**Passenger pigeons** once lived in North America in flocks of billions that darkened the sky. Heavy hunting and loss of forests wiped them out. The last one, named Martha, died in 1914 at the Cincinnati Zoo."],
  ["tell me about komodo dragons | komodo dragon facts | how big is a komodo dragon", "The **Komodo dragon** is the world's largest lizard, reaching about 3 meters (10 feet) long. It lives on a few islands in Indonesia and has a venomous bite. Female Komodo dragons can sometimes have babies without a male!"],
  ["tell me about iguanas | iguana facts | do iguanas have a third eye", "Yes! **Iguanas** have a light-sensing spot on top of their head called a **parietal eye** that helps them notice shadows from above. Green iguanas live in trees and eat plants. Marine iguanas in the Galápagos are the only lizards that swim in the sea to feed."],
  ["tell me about geckos | gecko facts | how do geckos walk on walls", "**Geckos** have millions of tiny hairs on their toes that stick to surfaces using very weak forces between molecules. Together they're strong enough to let geckos run up glass and across ceilings. Many geckos can't blink, so they lick their eyes clean!"],
  ["tell me about bearded dragons | bearded dragon facts | why do bearded dragons wave", "**Bearded dragons** are lizards from Australia and popular pets. They puff up and darken the spiky \"beard\" under their chin when they feel threatened. Waving a front arm is often a calm signal to show they are not a threat."],
  ["tell me about gila monsters | gila monster facts", "The **Gila monster** is a venomous lizard from the deserts of the southwestern United States and Mexico. It moves slowly and spends most of its life underground. A substance found in its saliva helped scientists create a medicine for diabetes."],
  ["tell me about horned lizards | horned lizard facts | which lizard shoots blood from its eyes", "Some **horned lizards** can squirt a stream of blood from the corners of their eyes to scare off predators like coyotes. The blood tastes bad to dogs and cats. They live in dry areas of North America and eat lots of ants."],
  ["tell me about frilled lizards | frilled lizard facts", "The **frilled lizard** of Australia and New Guinea opens a big umbrella-like frill around its neck and hisses to scare enemies. If that doesn't work, it runs away on its back legs!"],
  ["tell me about basilisk lizards | which lizard can run on water", "The **basilisk lizard** of Central and South America can run across water for a short distance! It slaps its big back feet down very fast, making air pockets that keep it from sinking."],
  ["tell me about thorny devils | thorny devil facts | how does a thorny devil drink", "The **thorny devil** is a spiky Australian desert lizard. Tiny grooves between its scales soak up water from damp sand and dew and carry it to its mouth. It also has a knob on its neck that acts like a fake head to fool predators."],
  ["how far can a chameleon's tongue reach | chameleon tongue facts | can chameleons move their eyes separately", "A chameleon's tongue can shoot out farther than its own body length in a split second to grab insects. Its eyes can move separately, so it can look in two directions at once."],
  ["tell me about caimans | caiman facts | what is a caiman", "**Caimans** are relatives of alligators that live in Central and South America. Most are smaller than alligators and crocodiles. The black caiman, though, is one of the biggest predators in the Amazon."],
  ["tell me about gharials | gharial facts | what is a gharial", "The **gharial** is a crocodile relative from India and Nepal with a very long, thin snout full of teeth that's perfect for catching fish. Adult males have a round bump on the end of the snout. Gharials are critically endangered."],
  ["tell me about galapagos tortoises | giant tortoise facts | how long do tortoises live", "**Galápagos giant tortoises** can weigh over 400 kg (880 pounds) and live more than 100 years. Their shell shapes differ from island to island, which helped Charles Darwin think about how animals adapt. Tortoises live on land, while most turtles live in water."],
  ["tell me about leatherback turtles | leatherback sea turtle facts | what is the biggest turtle", "The **leatherback** is the world's biggest turtle and can weigh more than 500 kg (1,100 pounds). Instead of a hard shell, it has a leathery back. It eats mostly jellyfish and can dive more than 1,000 meters deep."],
  ["tell me about tuataras | tuatara facts | what is a tuatara", "The **tuatara** of New Zealand looks like a lizard but is the last survivor of an ancient group of reptiles that lived alongside early dinosaurs. It grows slowly and can live over 100 years."],
  ["tell me about cobras | cobra facts | why do cobras have hoods", "**Cobras** spread their ribs to make a hood when they feel threatened, making themselves look bigger. They are venomous and live in Africa and Asia. Some spitting cobras can squirt venom toward an enemy's eyes, so always stay far away."],
  ["tell me about king cobras | king cobra facts", "The **king cobra** is the world's longest venomous snake, growing up to about 5.5 meters (18 feet). It mostly eats other snakes. Females are the only snakes known to build nests of leaves for their eggs."],
  ["tell me about pythons | python facts | what is the difference between a python and a boa", "**Pythons** and **boas** are both constrictors that squeeze their prey instead of using venom. Most pythons lay eggs and live in Africa, Asia and Australia, while most boas give birth to live babies and live in the Americas. Both can swallow prey much wider than their heads."],
  ["tell me about rattlesnakes | rattlesnake facts | can you tell a rattlesnake's age by its rattle", "No! A rattlesnake adds a new rattle segment each time it sheds its skin, which can happen several times a year, and old segments can break off. The rattle is made of keratin and buzzes as a warning to stay away. Rattlesnakes sense body heat with pits on their faces."],
  ["tell me about black mambas | black mamba facts | why is it called a black mamba", "The **black mamba** of Africa is actually grayish or olive-colored; it's named for the inky black inside of its mouth, which it shows when threatened. It's one of the fastest snakes and is very venomous, so it should always be left alone."],
  ["tell me about sea snakes | sea snake facts", "**Sea snakes** live in warm parts of the Indian and Pacific Oceans. They breathe air but can stay underwater a long time, and some can even take in a little oxygen through their skin. Their flat, paddle-shaped tails help them swim."],
  ["tell me about garter snakes | garter snake facts | are garter snakes dangerous", "**Garter snakes** are common, harmless snakes found across North America. In Manitoba, Canada, tens of thousands of them come out of winter dens at the same time each spring, one of the biggest snake gatherings in the world."],
  ["tell me about sidewinders | how does a sidewinder move", "A **sidewinder** is a desert rattlesnake that moves by throwing its body sideways in loops. Only a small part of its body touches the hot sand at a time, and it doesn't slip on loose sand."],
  ["tell me about salamanders | salamander facts | are salamanders lizards", "**Salamanders** look like lizards but are amphibians, with smooth, moist skin and no scales. Many can regrow lost legs and tails. Most live near water or in damp places under logs and rocks."],
  ["tell me about newts | newt facts | what is the difference between a newt and a salamander", "A **newt** is a kind of salamander. Newts usually have rougher skin and spend part of their lives in water and part on land. Some young newts, called **efts**, are bright orange and live on land for a few years."],
  ["tell me about caecilians | caecilian facts | what is a caecilian", "**Caecilians** are legless amphibians that look like big earthworms or small snakes. Most live underground in warm, wet places and have tiny eyes covered by skin. They are related to frogs and salamanders."],
  ["tell me about poison dart frogs | poison dart frog facts | why are poison dart frogs colorful", "**Poison dart frogs** live in Central and South American rainforests. Their bright colors warn predators that they are toxic. Their poison comes from the bugs they eat in the wild, so frogs raised in zoos on other food aren't poisonous."],
  ["tell me about red eyed tree frogs | red eyed tree frog facts", "The **red-eyed tree frog** lives in Central American rainforests. During the day it sleeps on leaves, hiding its bright colors. If disturbed, it suddenly pops open its huge red eyes, which can startle a predator long enough for the frog to escape."],
  ["tell me about bullfrogs | bullfrog facts", "The **American bullfrog** is the biggest frog in North America. Males make a deep call that sounds a bit like a cow mooing. Bullfrogs eat almost anything they can fit in their mouths."],
  ["tell me about cane toads | cane toad facts | why are cane toads a problem in australia", "**Cane toads** were brought to Australia in 1935 to eat beetles in sugar cane fields, but they didn't help much. Instead they spread across the country, and their poison harms native animals that try to eat them. It's a famous lesson about introducing species to new places."],
  ["tell me about glass frogs | glass frog facts | which frog is see through", "**Glass frogs** of Central and South America have see-through skin on their bellies, so you can see their hearts and other organs. While sleeping, they hide most of their red blood cells in their liver to become even more transparent."],
  ["tell me about goliath frogs | what is the biggest frog | goliath frog facts", "The **goliath frog** of West Africa is the biggest frog in the world. It can grow about 32 cm (over 1 foot) long and weigh over 3 kg (7 pounds). Some goliath frogs even move rocks to build pond nests for their eggs."],
  ["what is the biggest amphibian | tell me about the chinese giant salamander", "The **Chinese giant salamander** is the world's largest amphibian and can reach about 1.8 meters (6 feet) long. It lives in cool mountain streams in China and is critically endangered."],
  ["tell me about hellbenders | hellbender facts | what is a hellbender", "The **hellbender** is North America's largest salamander, living in clean, rocky streams. Its wrinkly skin folds help it absorb oxygen straight from the water. Its funny nicknames include \"snot otter.\""],
  ["tell me about olms | olm facts | what is an olm", "The **olm** is a blind, pale salamander that lives in dark underwater caves in Europe. It can live for about 100 years and survive for years without eating. People once thought olms were baby dragons!"],
  ["tell me about wood frogs | which frog can freeze solid", "The **wood frog** of North America can freeze nearly solid in winter! Its heart stops and it stops breathing, but special sugars protect its cells. In spring, it thaws out and hops away."],
  ["tell me about clownfish | clownfish facts | why don't anemones sting clownfish", "**Clownfish** live among the stinging tentacles of sea anemones, protected by a special slimy coating on their skin. The anemone keeps them safe, and the clownfish help clean and protect the anemone. All clownfish are born male, and the biggest in a group can turn female."],
  ["tell me about pufferfish | pufferfish facts | why do pufferfish puff up", "When threatened, a **pufferfish** quickly gulps water to puff into a spiky ball that's hard to swallow. Many pufferfish also contain a strong poison. Some male pufferfish draw beautiful circle patterns in the sand to attract females."],
  ["tell me about piranhas | piranha facts | are piranhas dangerous", "**Piranhas** live in South American rivers and have very sharp teeth, but their scary reputation is mostly exaggerated. Many eat insects, plants, seeds and fish scraps, and they're often more scared than scary. Some even make barking sounds!"],
  ["tell me about salmon | salmon facts | why do salmon swim upstream", "**Salmon** hatch in rivers, swim out to the ocean to grow, and then return to the very same stream where they were born to lay their eggs. They find their way using smell and possibly Earth's magnetic field. They leap up waterfalls and swim against strong currents to get there."],
  ["tell me about tuna | tuna facts | how big can tuna get", "**Tuna** are fast, powerful ocean fish that never stop swimming. The Atlantic bluefin tuna can weigh over 600 kg (1,300 pounds). Unlike most fish, tuna can keep parts of their bodies warmer than the water around them."],
  ["tell me about swordfish | swordfish facts | what do swordfish use their swords for", "A **swordfish** uses its long, flat bill to slash at schools of fish, making them easier to catch. It also has a special organ that warms its eyes and brain, helping it see well in cold, deep water."],
  ["tell me about stingrays | stingray facts | how do stingrays sting", "**Stingrays** are flat fish related to sharks, with skeletons made of cartilage. They have a sharp barb on their tail that they use only for defense. Beach-goers do the \"stingray shuffle,\" sliding their feet so rays have time to swim away."],
  ["tell me about manta rays | manta ray facts | do manta rays sting", "**Manta rays** are giant rays with a wingspan that can reach about 7 meters (23 feet). They don't have a stinger and are gentle filter feeders that eat tiny plankton. They also have one of the biggest brains of any fish."],
  ["tell me about eels | eel facts | are eels fish", "Yes, **eels** are fish, even though they look like snakes. European and American eels travel thousands of kilometers to the Sargasso Sea in the Atlantic Ocean to lay their eggs. Moray eels even have a second set of jaws in their throat!"],
  ["tell me about electric eels | electric eel facts | how do electric eels make electricity", "**Electric eels** aren't true eels; they're a kind of knifefish from South America. Special cells in their bodies work like batteries and can produce shocks of over 600 volts to stun prey or defend themselves. They also breathe air at the surface."],
  ["tell me about catfish | catfish facts | why are they called catfish", "**Catfish** are named for the long whisker-like feelers, called **barbels**, around their mouths. Their bodies are covered in taste buds, so they can practically taste with their whole skin. They live in rivers and lakes all over the world."],
  ["tell me about betta fish | betta fish facts | why can't betta fish live together", "**Betta fish**, or Siamese fighting fish, come from Southeast Asia. Males are territorial and will fight each other, so they should be kept alone. Bettas have a special organ that lets them breathe air from the surface, and males build nests of bubbles."],
  ["tell me about angelfish | angelfish facts", "Freshwater **angelfish** come from the Amazon and are popular aquarium fish with tall, triangle-shaped bodies. Marine angelfish are a different, very colorful group that live on coral reefs."],
  ["tell me about anglerfish | anglerfish facts | why do anglerfish have a light", "Many **anglerfish** live in the dark deep sea and have a glowing lure on their heads, like a fishing rod. Tiny glowing bacteria make the light, which attracts curious fish right to the anglerfish's huge mouth."],
  ["tell me about lionfish | lionfish facts | are lionfish venomous", "**Lionfish** are beautiful striped fish with long, venomous spines. They come from the Indian and Pacific Oceans, but they've spread into the Atlantic and Caribbean, where they eat many native reef fish."],
  ["tell me about flying fish | can fish fly | flying fish facts", "**Flying fish** can't really fly, but they can glide! They swim fast, burst out of the water and spread their wing-like fins, gliding up to about 200 meters to escape predators."],
  ["tell me about barracudas | barracuda facts", "**Barracudas** are long, fast ocean fish with fang-like teeth. They hunt by ambush, waiting quietly and then dashing forward at high speed. Shiny objects can catch their attention because they look like fish scales."],
  ["tell me about hammerhead sharks | hammerhead shark facts | why do hammerhead sharks have weird heads", "A **hammerhead shark's** wide head spreads out its eyes and special electricity-sensing organs, helping it scan the sea floor for prey like stingrays. Some kinds gather in big schools during the day."],
  ["tell me about great white sharks | great white shark facts | how big is a great white shark", "**Great white sharks** are the largest predatory fish, reaching about 6 meters (20 feet). They mostly eat seals, fish and dead whales. Attacks on people are very rare, and many are probably cases of mistaken identity."],
  ["tell me about tiger sharks | tiger shark facts", "**Tiger sharks** get their name from the dark stripes young sharks have, which fade as they grow. They'll eat almost anything, from fish and sea turtles to things that aren't food at all, which earned them the nickname \"garbage cans of the sea.\""],
  ["tell me about greenland sharks | greenland shark facts | how long do greenland sharks live", "**Greenland sharks** are slow-swimming sharks of the cold Arctic seas. Scientists estimate some live for several hundred years, making them the longest-living animals with backbones that we know of. They don't become adults until they're about 150 years old!"],
  ["tell me about goblin sharks | goblin shark facts", "The **goblin shark** is a strange pinkish deep-sea shark with a long flat snout. When it bites, its jaws shoot forward out of its mouth like a slingshot to grab prey."],
  ["tell me about mudskippers | mudskipper facts | can fish walk on land", "**Mudskippers** are fish that spend lots of time out of water on muddy shores! They use their fins like crutches to \"walk\" and skip, and they breathe through their skin and the lining of their mouths when out of water."],
  ["tell me about lungfish | lungfish facts | can fish breathe air", "**Lungfish** have lungs and can breathe air! When their ponds dry up, some lungfish dig into the mud, wrap themselves in a slimy cocoon and wait, sometimes for years, until the rain comes back."],
  ["tell me about coelacanths | coelacanth facts | what is a living fossil fish", "The **coelacanth** was thought to have died out with the dinosaurs, but one was caught alive off South Africa in 1938! It has lobe-shaped fins that move a bit like legs and is a relative of the fish that first came onto land."],
  ["tell me about sturgeon | sturgeon facts", "**Sturgeon** are ancient, large fish with bony plates instead of scales. Some can live over 100 years. Their eggs are used to make a fancy food called caviar, and overfishing has made many sturgeon endangered."],
  ["tell me about koi | koi fish facts | how long do koi live", "**Koi** are colorful pond fish bred from carp, especially in Japan. With good care they can live for decades, and some have lived much longer. People prize them for their red, orange, white, black and gold patterns."],
  ["tell me about parrotfish | parrotfish facts | do parrotfish make sand", "Yes! **Parrotfish** use their beak-like teeth to scrape algae off coral and grind up the hard coral bits. They poop out fine white sand, which helps build tropical beaches. Some sleep inside a bubble of mucus they make each night."],
  ["tell me about blobfish | blobfish facts | why does the blobfish look like that", "The **blobfish** lives deep off the coasts of Australia and New Zealand, where water pressure is huge. Down there it looks like a normal fish. It only turns into a droopy blob when brought up to the surface, where the pressure is much lower."],
  ["tell me about ocean sunfish | mola mola facts | what is the heaviest bony fish", "The **ocean sunfish**, or mola, is the heaviest bony fish in the world and can weigh over 2,000 kg (4,400 pounds). It looks like a giant swimming head and eats lots of jellyfish. Sunfish lie on their sides at the surface, maybe to warm up."],
  ["tell me about leafy seadragons | leafy seadragon facts", "The **leafy seadragon** is a relative of the seahorse from southern Australia. Leaf-shaped flaps all over its body make it look exactly like floating seaweed. Like seahorses, the dads carry the eggs."],
  ["tell me about archerfish | archerfish facts | which fish spits water", "**Archerfish** spit a jet of water to knock insects off leaves above the water, then gobble them up. They can adjust their aim for the way light bends at the water's surface."],
  ["tell me about remoras | remora facts | why do fish stick to sharks", "A **remora** has a sucker on top of its head that lets it hitch a ride on sharks, whales, rays and turtles. It gets free transport and eats scraps and parasites, without harming its host."],
  ["tell me about oarfish | oarfish facts | what is the longest bony fish", "The **oarfish** is the longest bony fish in the world, growing up to about 8 meters (26 feet) or more. It's a silvery, ribbon-like deep-sea fish with a red crest, and it may have inspired old sea serpent stories."],
  ["tell me about guppies | guppy facts", "**Guppies** are small, colorful freshwater fish that are easy pets. Instead of laying eggs, they give birth to live babies called fry. Males are usually much more colorful than females."],
  ["tell me about cleaner wrasse | cleaner fish facts | what is a cleaning station", "**Cleaner wrasses** are small fish that set up \"cleaning stations\" on coral reefs. Bigger fish line up and let the wrasses eat the parasites off their skin and even inside their mouths, without eating them."],
  ["tell me about stonefish | stonefish facts", "The **stonefish** looks exactly like a rock or lump of coral, which helps it ambush prey. It's one of the most venomous fish, with spines on its back, so people in the tropics wear water shoes on reefs."],
  ["tell me about hagfish | hagfish facts | why do hagfish make slime", "**Hagfish** are eel-shaped ocean creatures that can release a huge amount of slime in seconds to clog the gills of predators. To clean off, they tie themselves into a knot and slide it down their bodies!"],
  ["tell me about dragonflies | dragonfly facts | how good are dragonflies at hunting", "**Dragonflies** are some of the best hunters in the animal kingdom, catching most of the insects they chase. Their huge eyes see almost all the way around them, and they can fly forward, backward and hover. Their relatives were flying before the dinosaurs."],
  ["tell me about grasshoppers | grasshopper facts | how do grasshoppers make noise", "**Grasshoppers** make sounds by rubbing their back legs against their wings. They hear with ear-like organs on the sides of their bellies. Their strong back legs let them jump many times their own body length."],
  ["tell me about crickets | cricket insect facts | why do crickets chirp", "Male **crickets** chirp by rubbing their wings together to attract females. Their ears are on their front legs! Crickets chirp faster when it's warm, so you can even estimate the temperature by counting their chirps."],
  ["tell me about praying mantises | praying mantis facts | why is it called a praying mantis", "A **praying mantis** holds its spiky front legs folded as if praying, ready to snatch prey. It can turn its head almost all the way around to look over its shoulder. Mantises are patient ambush hunters that eat other insects."],
  ["tell me about stick insects | stick insect facts | walking stick facts", "**Stick insects** look just like twigs, which keeps birds from spotting them. Some sway back and forth like a branch in the wind. A few kinds are among the longest insects in the world, more than 50 cm (20 inches) long with legs stretched out."],
  ["tell me about dung beetles | dung beetle facts | why do dung beetles roll poop", "**Dung beetles** roll balls of animal poop to eat and to lay their eggs in. Some can roll balls much heavier than themselves, and some even steer at night using the light of the Milky Way. They help recycle nutrients and keep fields clean."],
  ["tell me about beetles | beetle facts | how many kinds of beetles are there", "**Beetles** are the biggest group of animals on Earth, with over 350,000 known kinds. They have hard front wings that cover and protect their flying wings. Ladybugs, fireflies and weevils are all beetles!"],
  ["tell me about rhinoceros beetles | rhino beetle facts | what is the strongest insect", "**Rhinoceros beetles** have big horns used to wrestle other males. For their size, they are some of the strongest animals on Earth and can lift many times their own body weight. The Hercules beetle is one of the longest beetles."],
  ["tell me about fireflies | firefly facts | why do fireflies glow", "**Fireflies** are beetles that glow using a chemical reaction in their bellies, called **bioluminescence**. Each kind flashes its own pattern of light to find a mate. Their light makes almost no heat, so it's called \"cold light.\""],
  ["tell me about mosquitoes | mosquito facts | why do mosquitoes bite", "Only female **mosquitoes** bite, because they need protein from blood to make eggs. Males sip flower nectar instead. Mosquitoes find people by sensing the carbon dioxide we breathe out, plus body heat and smells."],
  ["tell me about flies | housefly facts | do flies taste with their feet", "Yes, **houseflies** have taste sensors on their feet! They have big compound eyes made of thousands of tiny lenses, which is why they're so hard to swat. They can't chew, so they soak up liquid food with a spongy mouth."],
  ["tell me about termites | termite facts | how do termites build mounds", "**Termites** live in huge colonies with a queen who can lay thousands of eggs a day. Some African termites build towering mounds with tunnels that work like air conditioning, and architects have copied the idea for buildings. Tiny microbes in their guts help them digest wood."],
  ["tell me about cockroaches | cockroach facts | can a cockroach live without its head", "Surprisingly, a **cockroach** can live for about a week without its head, because it breathes through little holes along its body. Cockroaches have been around for hundreds of millions of years. Of the thousands of kinds, only a few live in people's homes."],
  ["what is the difference between a moth and a butterfly | moth vs butterfly | tell me about moths", "**Moths** usually fly at night, have feathery or thin antennae and rest with wings spread flat. **Butterflies** usually fly during the day, have club-tipped antennae and rest with wings folded up. There are many more kinds of moths than butterflies!"],
  ["tell me about luna moths | luna moth facts", "The **luna moth** is a big pale-green moth from North America with long tails on its wings. Adults don't have mouths and can't eat, so they live only about a week, just long enough to find a mate."],
  ["tell me about monarch butterflies | monarch butterfly facts | where do monarch butterflies migrate", "Every fall, millions of **monarch butterflies** fly from Canada and the United States to mountain forests in Mexico, a trip of up to about 4,800 km (3,000 miles). It takes several generations to make the round trip. Monarchs eat milkweed as caterpillars, which makes them taste bad to birds."],
  ["tell me about wasps | wasp facts | what is the difference between a bee and a wasp", "**Wasps** are usually thinner and smoother than bees, with a narrow waist. Many build paper nests by chewing wood into pulp. Wasps can sting more than once, but they also help by eating pest insects and pollinating some flowers."],
  ["tell me about hornets | hornet facts", "**Hornets** are the biggest kinds of wasps. They build large paper nests in trees or hollow spaces and hunt other insects to feed their young. They defend their nests, so it's best to keep your distance."],
  ["tell me about bumblebees | bumblebee facts | what is the difference between a bumblebee and a honeybee", "**Bumblebees** are bigger and fuzzier than honeybees and live in small colonies that usually last just one season. They can fly in cooler weather and shake flowers with a buzz to release pollen. Honeybees live in huge colonies and store lots of honey."],
  ["tell me about cicadas | cicada facts | why do cicadas come out every 17 years", "Some **periodical cicadas** spend 13 or 17 years underground as young nymphs, sipping juices from tree roots. Then millions come out at once, which means predators can't eat them all. Male cicadas make loud buzzing songs using drum-like parts on their bodies."],
  ["tell me about silkworms | silkworm facts | how is silk made", "**Silkworms** are caterpillars of the silk moth. Each one spins a cocoon from a single silk thread that can be hundreds of meters long. People in China discovered how to make silk cloth thousands of years ago."],
  ["tell me about aphids | aphid facts | why do ants farm aphids", "**Aphids** are tiny insects that suck sap from plants. They make a sweet liquid called honeydew, so some ants protect them and \"milk\" them like tiny cows. Many aphids can have babies without a mate, so their numbers grow very fast."],
  ["tell me about locusts | locust facts | what is a locust swarm", "**Locusts** are a kind of grasshopper that can change behavior when crowded. They gather into huge swarms of millions or even billions that can travel long distances and eat crops along the way."],
  ["tell me about fleas | flea facts | how high can fleas jump", "**Fleas** are tiny wingless insects that can jump around 100 times their body length thanks to a springy protein in their legs. They live on animals and drink blood, which is why pets often get flea treatments."],
  ["tell me about water striders | how do water striders walk on water", "**Water striders** walk on water by spreading their weight over long legs covered in tiny water-repelling hairs. The surface tension of the water holds them up like a stretchy skin."],
  ["tell me about earwigs | earwig facts | do earwigs crawl into ears", "No, **earwigs** don't crawl into people's ears; that's an old myth. They're harmless insects with pincers on their tail ends that they use for defense and for courtship. Earwig moms take good care of their eggs and babies."],
  ["tell me about leafcutter ants | leafcutter ant facts | what do leafcutter ants do with leaves", "**Leafcutter ants** cut pieces of leaves and carry them home, but they don't eat the leaves. Instead, they use them to grow a special fungus garden, and the fungus is their food. Some colonies have millions of ants."],
  ["tell me about army ants | army ant facts", "**Army ants** don't build permanent nests. They march in huge columns to hunt insects and other small animals, and they can link their bodies together to build living bridges and shelters."],
  ["tell me about atlas moths | atlas moth facts | what is the biggest moth", "The **Atlas moth** of Asia is one of the biggest moths in the world, with wings up to about 25 cm (10 inches) across. The tips of its wings look a bit like snake heads, which may scare away predators."],
  ["tell me about wetas | weta facts | what is a weta", "**Wetas** are big cricket-like insects from New Zealand. The giant weta is one of the heaviest insects in the world, sometimes weighing more than a mouse!"],
  ["tell me about pill bugs | roly poly facts | are pill bugs insects", "**Pill bugs**, or roly-polies, aren't insects; they're land crustaceans, related to crabs and shrimp! They breathe with gill-like parts, so they need damp places. When scared, they roll into a little ball."],
  ["tell me about millipedes | millipede facts | how many legs does a millipede have", "**Millipedes** have two pairs of legs on most body segments. The leggiest known millipede has over 1,300 legs! Most millipedes eat dead leaves and are slow, while centipedes are fast hunters."],
  ["tell me about scorpions | scorpion facts | why do scorpions glow under uv light", "**Scorpions** are arachnids, related to spiders. They glow blue-green under ultraviolet light, and scientists still aren't completely sure why. Scorpion moms carry their babies on their backs until the babies are big enough."],
  ["tell me about tarantulas | tarantula facts | are tarantulas dangerous", "**Tarantulas** are big, hairy spiders, but their venom is usually no worse than a bee sting for people. Some flick tiny itchy hairs from their bellies to defend themselves. Female tarantulas can live 20 years or more!"],
  ["tell me about black widow spiders | black widow facts", "Female **black widows** are shiny black with a red hourglass shape on their bellies. Their venom is strong, but they're shy and rarely bite unless pressed or trapped. Males are much smaller and less noticeable."],
  ["tell me about jumping spiders | jumping spider facts", "**Jumping spiders** have some of the best eyesight of any spider, with two huge front eyes. Instead of building webs to trap prey, they stalk and pounce. Peacock spiders, a type of jumping spider, do colorful dances!"],
  ["tell me about ticks | tick facts | are ticks insects", "**Ticks** aren't insects; they're arachnids with eight legs, like spiders. They wait on grass and plants to climb onto animals and drink blood. After hiking in long grass or woods, it's smart to check your skin and pets for ticks."],
  ["what is a daddy longlegs | are daddy longlegs spiders | daddy long legs facts", "The name \"daddy longlegs\" is used for different creatures. One kind, called **harvestmen**, are arachnids but not spiders: they have one oval body part, don't make silk and have no venom. Cellar spiders with long, thin legs are also called daddy longlegs."],
  ["tell me about orb weaver spiders | orb weaver facts | how do spiders make webs", "Spiders make silk inside their bodies and pull it out of spinnerets at the back. **Orb weavers** build the classic round spiral web, first making spokes and then a sticky spiral. Many eat their old web each day to recycle the silk."],
  ["tell me about camel spiders | camel spider facts", "**Camel spiders** live in deserts and look like spiders, but they belong to a different group of arachnids. Lots of scary stories about them are made up; they don't chase people and they aren't venomous. They're fast hunters of insects."],
  ["tell me about horseshoe crabs | horseshoe crab facts | are horseshoe crabs crabs", "**Horseshoe crabs** aren't true crabs; they're more closely related to spiders and scorpions. They've been around for hundreds of millions of years, long before dinosaurs. Their blue blood is used to help test medicines and vaccines for safety."],
  ["tell me about crabs | crab facts | why do crabs walk sideways", "Most **crabs** walk sideways because their legs bend at the joints that way, so moving sideways is quicker and easier. Crabs have ten legs, including two claws, and a hard shell called an exoskeleton that they shed as they grow."],
  ["tell me about hermit crabs | hermit crab facts | why do hermit crabs change shells", "**Hermit crabs** have soft bellies, so they live inside empty snail shells. As they grow, they move into bigger shells. Sometimes many crabs line up by size and swap shells one after another!"],
  ["tell me about lobsters | lobster facts | what color is lobster blood", "**Lobsters** have bluish blood because it uses copper instead of iron to carry oxygen. They can regrow lost claws and legs, and some live for decades. Most lobsters are greenish-brown and only turn red when cooked."],
  ["tell me about shrimp | shrimp facts | what is a pistol shrimp", "**Shrimp** are small crustaceans found in oceans and fresh water. The **pistol shrimp** snaps its claw so fast that it shoots out a bubble that pops with a loud crack, stunning small fish nearby."],
  ["tell me about krill | krill facts | what do whales eat", "**Krill** are tiny shrimp-like animals that live in huge swarms. They're a main food of blue whales, penguins, seals and many fish. Antarctic krill are one of the most plentiful animals on Earth when you add up their total weight."],
  ["tell me about barnacles | barnacle facts | are barnacles animals", "Yes! **Barnacles** are crustaceans, related to crabs. As babies they swim, then glue their heads to rocks, boats or even whales and build a hard shell. They catch food by kicking their feathery legs out into the water."],
  ["tell me about mantis shrimp | mantis shrimp facts | how hard can a mantis shrimp punch", "**Mantis shrimp** punch with clubs on their front limbs so fast that they can smash snail shells, and big ones can even crack aquarium glass. Their eyes are among the most complex in the animal world, with many more kinds of color sensors than ours."],
  ["tell me about crayfish | crayfish facts | what is a crawdad", "**Crayfish**, also called crawdads or crawfish, look like small lobsters but live in fresh water like streams and ponds. They hide under rocks during the day and walk backward quickly when scared."],
  ["tell me about coconut crabs | coconut crab facts | what is the biggest land crab", "The **coconut crab** is the biggest land-living arthropod in the world, with legs spanning up to about 1 meter (3 feet). It can climb trees and crack open coconuts with its powerful claws."],
  ["tell me about japanese spider crabs | which crab has the longest legs", "The **Japanese spider crab** has the longest legs of any arthropod, with a leg span of up to about 3.7 meters (12 feet) from claw to claw. It lives in deep waters around Japan."],
  ["tell me about fiddler crabs | fiddler crab facts", "Male **fiddler crabs** have one super-big claw that they wave to attract females and warn rivals. They live on beaches and mudflats and dig burrows in the sand."],
  ["tell me about squid | squid facts | how do squid swim", "**Squid** swim by squirting water out of a tube, like jet propulsion. They have eight arms, two long tentacles and three hearts, and they can squirt ink to escape. Their mouths have a sharp beak like a parrot's."],
  ["tell me about giant squid | giant squid facts | how big is a giant squid", "**Giant squid** can grow about 12 meters (40 feet) long, including their tentacles. They have the biggest eyes of any animal, about the size of a dinner plate or basketball. They live deep in the ocean and were first filmed alive in the wild in the 2000s."],
  ["tell me about colossal squid | colossal squid facts | what is the heaviest invertebrate", "The **colossal squid** is the heaviest invertebrate known, weighing up to about 500 kg (1,100 pounds). It lives in the cold, deep waters around Antarctica and has sharp swiveling hooks on its arms."],
  ["tell me about cuttlefish | cuttlefish facts | is a cuttlefish a fish", "No, **cuttlefish** aren't fish; they're mollusks related to squid and octopuses. They can change color and texture in a flash to hide or communicate. Inside, they have a light shell called a **cuttlebone** that helps them float, and their pupils are shaped like a W."],
  ["tell me about nautiluses | nautilus facts | what is a nautilus", "The **nautilus** is a mollusk that lives in a beautiful spiral shell divided into chambers. It controls gas and liquid in the chambers to float up and down. Nautiluses have changed little for millions of years and have dozens of small tentacles."],
  ["tell me about clams | giant clam facts | how big can a clam get", "**Giant clams** of the Indian and Pacific Oceans can weigh over 200 kg (440 pounds) and live for more than 100 years. Tiny algae living in their colorful lips make food from sunlight and share it with the clam."],
  ["tell me about oysters | oyster facts | how do oysters make pearls", "When something like a parasite or bit of grit gets inside an **oyster**, it coats the irritating object with layers of shiny material called **nacre**. Over years, that builds up into a pearl. Oysters also clean water by filtering it as they feed."],
  ["tell me about mussels | mussel facts", "**Mussels** attach to rocks using strong threads called byssal threads that stick even underwater. Scientists study mussel \"glue\" to invent new medical glues. Mussels filter tiny food from the water."],
  ["tell me about scallops | scallop facts | do scallops have eyes", "Yes! **Scallops** can have up to about 200 tiny eyes along the edge of their shells. Unlike most clams, scallops can swim by clapping their shells together to shoot water out."],
  ["tell me about slugs | slug facts | what is the difference between a slug and a snail", "A **slug** is basically a snail without a big outer shell. Without a shell, slugs need damp places to keep from drying out, and their slime helps them move. The banana slug of North America can be bright yellow!"],
  ["tell me about nudibranchs | sea slug facts | what is a nudibranch", "**Nudibranchs** are sea slugs, many with dazzling colors and patterns. Their bright colors warn predators that they taste bad or are toxic. Some even steal stinging cells from the jellyfish-like animals they eat and use them for defense."],
  ["what is coral | is coral an animal | tell me about coral reefs", "Yes, **coral** is an animal! Coral reefs are built by tiny animals called polyps that make hard skeletons, and tiny algae living inside them give them food and color. Reefs are home to about a quarter of all ocean species, even though they cover a tiny part of the sea floor."],
  ["how big is the great barrier reef | tell me about the great barrier reef", "The **Great Barrier Reef** off northeastern Australia is the largest coral reef system in the world, stretching over 2,000 km (1,400 miles). It's so big it can be seen from space. Thousands of kinds of fish, corals, turtles and other animals live there."],
  ["what is coral bleaching | why do corals turn white", "When ocean water gets too warm, corals push out the colorful algae living inside them and turn white. This is called **coral bleaching**. Corals can recover if the water cools down soon, but without the algae they can starve."],
  ["tell me about sea anemones | sea anemone facts | are sea anemones plants", "**Sea anemones** look like flowers, but they're animals related to jellyfish and corals. They catch food with stinging tentacles and mostly stay in one spot, though some can slowly slide around."],
  ["tell me about sea urchins | sea urchin facts", "**Sea urchins** are spiny, ball-shaped relatives of starfish. They move using their spines and tiny tube feet, and they scrape food with a mouth made of five teeth. They're an important food for sea otters."],
  ["tell me about sea cucumbers | sea cucumber facts", "**Sea cucumbers** are soft relatives of starfish that crawl on the sea floor, cleaning sand and mud as they eat. When threatened, some can push out sticky threads or even parts of their insides, then regrow them later."],
  ["tell me about sea sponges | are sponges animals | sponge facts", "Yes, **sponges** are animals, among the simplest on Earth. They have no brain, heart or muscles. Water flows through tiny holes in their bodies, and they filter out bits of food."],
  ["tell me about sand dollars | sand dollar facts | are sand dollars alive", "**Sand dollars** are flat, burrowing sea urchins. Living ones are covered in tiny fuzzy spines and are usually purple or brown. The white ones you find on beaches are their skeletons, left after they've died."],
  ["what is a portuguese man o war | is a portuguese man o war a jellyfish", "The **Portuguese man o' war** isn't a true jellyfish. It's a colony of many tiny animals working together as one, with a gas-filled float on top. Its long tentacles have a painful sting, even when washed up on a beach."],
  ["tell me about box jellyfish | box jellyfish facts", "**Box jellyfish** are cube-shaped jellies found in warm seas, including around northern Australia. Some are among the most venomous animals in the ocean. They also have 24 eyes and can swim actively instead of just drifting."],
  ["what is the biggest jellyfish | tell me about lion's mane jellyfish", "The **lion's mane jellyfish** is the largest known jellyfish. Its bell can be over 2 meters across, and its tentacles can stretch more than 30 meters (100 feet), longer than a blue whale!"],
  ["what is the immortal jellyfish | can a jellyfish live forever", "The **immortal jellyfish** can turn itself back into an earlier, baby-like stage when it's injured or stressed, and then grow up again. In theory this could go on and on, though in nature most still get eaten or get sick."],
  ["what is plankton | tell me about plankton", "**Plankton** are tiny living things that drift in water. Plant-like plankton make food from sunlight and produce a huge share of the oxygen we breathe, while animal plankton eat them. Plankton are the base of most ocean food chains."],
  ["what animals live in the deep sea | deep sea creatures | what lives at the bottom of the ocean", "The deep sea is dark, cold and has crushing pressure, but it's full of life! Anglerfish, giant squid, vampire squid, dumbo octopuses, giant isopods, gulper eels and tube worms all live there. Many deep-sea animals make their own light."],
  ["tell me about giant isopods | giant isopod facts", "**Giant isopods** are deep-sea relatives of pill bugs that can grow up to about 50 cm (20 inches) long. They scavenge on dead animals that sink to the sea floor and can go for years between big meals."],
  ["tell me about gulper eels | gulper eel facts | what is a pelican eel", "The **gulper eel**, also called the pelican eel, is a deep-sea fish with an enormous, stretchy mouth compared to its thin body. It can open wide like a net to scoop up small prey."],
  ["tell me about vampire squid | vampire squid facts | is a vampire squid scary", "Despite its name, the **vampire squid** is small, gentle and isn't a true squid or octopus. It lives in deep, low-oxygen water and eats bits of drifting dead stuff called marine snow. When threatened, it pulls its webbed arms over itself like a cloak."],
  ["tell me about dumbo octopuses | dumbo octopus facts", "**Dumbo octopuses** are deep-sea octopuses with fins that look like the ears of Dumbo the elephant. They flap these fins to hover. They live deeper than any other known octopus, thousands of meters down."],
  ["tell me about viperfish | viperfish facts", "The **viperfish** is a deep-sea fish with fang-like teeth so long they don't fit inside its mouth. It has glowing spots on its body to attract prey in the dark."],
  ["tell me about yeti crabs | yeti crab facts", "**Yeti crabs** live near hot water vents on the deep sea floor. Their arms are covered in fuzzy hairs where they grow bacteria, which they then eat. Some even wave their arms to help the bacteria grow!"],
  ["tell me about giant tube worms | tube worm facts | what lives at hydrothermal vents", "**Giant tube worms** live at deep-sea hot vents and can grow over 2 meters (7 feet) long. They have no mouth or stomach; bacteria inside them make food from chemicals in the vent water. Their red tips get color from blood rich in hemoglobin."],
  ["tell me about barreleye fish | barreleye fish facts | which fish has a see through head", "The **barreleye fish** has a see-through, dome-shaped head! Its tube-shaped green eyes are inside the clear dome and can rotate to look up or forward."],
  ["what is a siphonophore | what is the longest animal", "A **siphonophore** is a colony of many tiny animals that live and work together like one creature. Some deep-sea siphonophores can stretch over 40 meters (130 feet), making them among the longest animals in the world."],
  ["what is bioluminescence | why do some animals glow", "**Bioluminescence** is light made by living things through a chemical reaction. Fireflies, anglerfish, some jellyfish and many deep-sea animals use it to find mates, lure food, confuse predators or hide. Most living light in the ocean is blue."],
  ["what is marine snow | what do deep sea animals eat", "**Marine snow** is a steady \"snowfall\" of tiny bits of dead plankton, poop and other material drifting down from the ocean's surface. Many deep-sea animals depend on it for food, along with the bodies of whales and fish that sink."],
  ["tell me about frilled sharks | frilled shark facts", "The **frilled shark** is an eel-like deep-sea shark with about 300 needle-sharp teeth in rows. It looks so ancient that people call it a \"living fossil.\" Frilled gills give it its name."],
  ["tell me about labrador retrievers | labrador facts | are labs good family dogs", "**Labrador Retrievers** are friendly, gentle, energetic dogs and one of the most popular breeds in the world. They were bred to fetch for fishermen and hunters, so they love swimming and playing fetch. Labs are great family dogs and are often trained as guide dogs."],
  ["tell me about golden retrievers | golden retriever facts", "**Golden Retrievers** are kind, patient and eager to please, which makes them wonderful family pets and therapy dogs. They have soft, golden coats that shed a lot. They love to carry things in their mouths, from toys to socks!"],
  ["tell me about german shepherds | german shepherd facts", "**German Shepherds** are smart, loyal and brave dogs. Because they learn quickly and have great noses, they often work as police, search-and-rescue and guide dogs. They need lots of exercise and training."],
  ["tell me about poodles | poodle facts | are poodles smart", "**Poodles** are among the smartest dog breeds. They come in three sizes: standard, miniature and toy. Their curly coats shed very little, and their fancy haircut started as a way to help them swim while keeping their joints warm."],
  ["tell me about bulldogs | bulldog facts", "**Bulldogs** are sturdy, wrinkly dogs with pushed-in faces. They're usually calm, gentle and love naps. Because of their short snouts, they can get too hot easily and need care in warm weather."],
  ["tell me about french bulldogs | french bulldog facts | frenchie facts", "**French Bulldogs** are small, playful dogs with big bat-like ears. They're affectionate, don't need tons of exercise, and are popular city pets. Like other flat-faced dogs, they shouldn't get too hot or exercise hard in the heat."],
  ["tell me about beagles | beagle facts", "**Beagles** are cheerful, curious hounds with amazing noses. They were bred to track rabbits and still love following scents. Beagles make a special howl called a **bay**, and they're great with kids."],
  ["tell me about chihuahuas | chihuahua facts | what is the smallest dog breed", "The **Chihuahua** is usually considered the smallest dog breed, often weighing just 1 to 3 kg (2 to 6 pounds). Named after a state in Mexico, Chihuahuas are bold, loyal and often bond strongly with one person."],
  ["tell me about dachshunds | dachshund facts | why are dachshunds called wiener dogs", "**Dachshunds** are long, low dogs nicknamed \"wiener dogs\" or \"sausage dogs\" because of their shape. Their name means \"badger dog\" in German, because they were bred to dig into badger burrows. They're brave, curious and a little stubborn."],
  ["tell me about huskies | siberian husky facts | why do huskies have blue eyes", "**Siberian Huskies** were bred to pull sleds over long, snowy distances. Many have bright blue eyes, or one blue and one brown, because of their genes. They're friendly and full of energy, and they're known for \"talking\" with howls instead of barking much."],
  ["tell me about border collies | border collie facts | what is the smartest dog breed", "**Border Collies** are often called the smartest dog breed. They were bred to herd sheep and can learn many words and commands. They need lots of exercise and brain games, or they get bored."],
  ["tell me about corgis | corgi facts", "**Corgis** are short-legged herding dogs from Wales. Their low height helped them nip at cattle's heels and dodge kicks. Pembroke Welsh Corgis became famous as favorites of Queen Elizabeth II."],
  ["tell me about pugs | pug facts", "**Pugs** are small, wrinkly-faced dogs from China, once loved by emperors. They're playful, affectionate clowns that love being near people. Their flat faces mean they can snore and get overheated easily."],
  ["tell me about great danes | great dane facts | what is the tallest dog breed", "**Great Danes** are one of the tallest dog breeds; when standing on their back legs, many are taller than a person. Despite their size, they're usually gentle giants, sometimes called \"Apollo of dogs.\""],
  ["tell me about shih tzus | shih tzu facts", "**Shih Tzus** are small, friendly lap dogs from Tibet and China. Their name means \"little lion.\" They have long, silky hair that needs regular brushing or a short \"puppy cut.\""],
  ["tell me about dalmatians | dalmatian facts | are dalmatians born with spots", "No, **Dalmatian** puppies are born pure white, and their spots appear over the first few weeks. Dalmatians used to run alongside horse-drawn fire engines, which is why they're linked to firefighters. They're energetic and need lots of exercise."],
  ["tell me about rottweilers | rottweiler facts", "**Rottweilers** are strong, confident dogs originally used to herd cattle and pull carts in Germany. With good training, they're loyal, calm and protective family dogs."],
  ["tell me about yorkshire terriers | yorkie facts", "**Yorkshire Terriers**, or Yorkies, are tiny dogs with long, silky coats. They were first bred in England to catch rats in mills. They're bold and lively, with a big personality in a small body."],
  ["tell me about boxers | boxer dog facts", "**Boxers** are muscular, playful dogs from Germany that stay puppy-like for years. They sometimes stand on their back legs and bat with their front paws, a bit like a boxer. They're great with families."],
  ["tell me about greyhounds | greyhound facts | how fast is a greyhound", "**Greyhounds** are the fastest dog breed and can run about 70 km/h (43 mph). Even so, they're calm at home and are sometimes called \"40 mph couch potatoes\" because they love lounging."],
  ["tell me about saint bernards | saint bernard facts", "**Saint Bernards** are huge, gentle dogs from the Swiss Alps. Monks used them to help find travelers lost in the snow. The famous image of them carrying little barrels around their necks is mostly a legend."],
  ["tell me about australian shepherds | australian shepherd facts", "Despite their name, **Australian Shepherds** were mainly developed in the United States to herd livestock. They're very smart and active and often have striking blue or mixed-colored eyes."],
  ["tell me about pomeranians | pomeranian facts", "**Pomeranians** are tiny, fluffy dogs related to bigger sled-pulling breeds. They're lively and curious and have a thick double coat that makes them look like a puffball."],
  ["tell me about dobermans | doberman facts", "**Doberman Pinschers** are sleek, athletic dogs first bred in Germany by a tax collector who wanted a protector. They're loyal, smart and easy to train, and many are big softies with their families."],
  ["tell me about cocker spaniels | cocker spaniel facts", "**Cocker Spaniels** are gentle, happy dogs with long, wavy ears. They were bred to help hunters flush out birds called woodcocks. They love people and need regular grooming."],
  ["tell me about bernese mountain dogs | bernese mountain dog facts", "**Bernese Mountain Dogs** are big, fluffy tri-colored dogs from Switzerland. They were farm dogs that pulled carts and guarded farms. They're calm, sweet and love cold weather."],
  ["tell me about jack russell terriers | jack russell facts", "**Jack Russell Terriers** are small, super-energetic dogs bred to hunt foxes. They're clever, fearless and can jump surprisingly high. They need lots of activity to stay happy."],
  ["tell me about shiba inus | shiba inu facts", "The **Shiba Inu** is an ancient breed from Japan with a fox-like face and curly tail. Shibas are independent, clean and a bit cat-like. When upset they can make a loud cry called the \"Shiba scream.\""],
  ["tell me about samoyeds | samoyed facts | why do samoyeds smile", "**Samoyeds** are fluffy white dogs from Siberia that herded reindeer and pulled sleds. The upturned corners of their mouths make it look like they're always smiling, which also helps keep drool from freezing on their faces."],
  ["tell me about basset hounds | basset hound facts", "**Basset Hounds** have short legs, long droopy ears and one of the best noses of any dog. Their long ears help sweep scents up toward their nose while they track. They're laid-back and friendly."],
  ["what dog breed is best for kids | what is a good family dog | best dog for a family", "Many breeds can be great with kids, like Labrador Retrievers, Golden Retrievers, Beagles and Cavalier King Charles Spaniels. The best dog depends on your family's space, time and energy. Ask a vet or shelter, and remember mixed-breed rescue dogs can be wonderful too!"],
  ["what is a mixed breed dog | what is a mutt", "A **mixed-breed dog**, or mutt, has parents from different breeds. Mutts can be healthy, smart and loving, and every one is unique. Lots of amazing mixed-breed dogs are waiting for homes at animal shelters."],
  ["tell me about persian cats | persian cat facts", "**Persian cats** have long, fluffy coats and sweet, flat faces. They're calm and gentle and enjoy relaxing in quiet homes. Their thick fur needs daily brushing to stay tangle-free."],
  ["tell me about siamese cats | siamese cat facts | why do siamese cats have dark ears", "**Siamese cats** have slim bodies, blue eyes and darker fur on their ears, face, paws and tail. Their color depends on temperature: cooler parts of the body grow darker fur. They're very chatty and love attention."],
  ["tell me about maine coon cats | maine coon facts | what is the biggest cat breed", "The **Maine Coon** is one of the biggest house cat breeds. It has a shaggy coat, tufted ears and a big bushy tail, perfect for cold winters in Maine, USA. They're friendly giants, sometimes called \"dogs of the cat world.\""],
  ["tell me about ragdoll cats | ragdoll cat facts | why are they called ragdoll cats", "**Ragdoll cats** are named for the way many go limp and relaxed when picked up, like a rag doll. They're large, gentle, blue-eyed cats that often follow their owners around."],
  ["tell me about bengal cats | bengal cat facts", "**Bengal cats** have spotted or marbled coats that make them look like tiny leopards. They come from crossing house cats with the wild Asian leopard cat. Bengals are very active and many even like water!"],
  ["tell me about sphynx cats | sphynx cat facts | why are some cats hairless", "The **Sphynx** cat looks hairless because of a natural gene change, though it usually has a fine peach-fuzz. Without much fur, Sphynx cats get cold easily and need baths to remove skin oils. They're super friendly and love warm laps."],
  ["tell me about british shorthair cats | british shorthair facts", "**British Shorthairs** are sturdy cats with round faces, thick plush coats and big copper or gold eyes. The blue-gray ones are especially famous. They're calm, easygoing and not too clingy."],
  ["tell me about scottish fold cats | scottish fold facts", "**Scottish Folds** have ears that fold forward and down, which comes from a gene that also affects their cartilage. That gene can cause painful joint problems, so many vets and breeders have health concerns about the breed."],
  ["tell me about abyssinian cats | abyssinian cat facts", "**Abyssinians** are slim, active cats with ticked coats, meaning each hair has bands of color. They're curious, playful and love to climb high places."],
  ["tell me about russian blue cats | russian blue facts", "**Russian Blues** have silvery blue-gray coats and bright green eyes. They're gentle, quiet and a bit shy with strangers but very loyal to their families."],
  ["tell me about norwegian forest cats | norwegian forest cat facts", "**Norwegian Forest Cats** have thick, water-resistant coats and bushy tails made for cold Scandinavian winters. They're excellent climbers and appear in old Norse stories."],
  ["what is a tabby cat | is tabby a breed", "**Tabby** isn't a breed; it's a coat pattern with stripes, swirls or spots and often an \"M\" shape on the forehead. Lots of different breeds and mixed cats can be tabbies."],
  ["why are calico cats almost always female | what is a calico cat", "A **calico** cat has patches of white, black and orange. The genes for orange and black fur are carried on the X chromosome, and you usually need two X chromosomes, which females have, to show both colors. Male calicos are very rare."],
  ["what is a tuxedo cat | is a tuxedo cat a breed", "A **tuxedo cat** is a black-and-white cat whose markings look like a fancy tuxedo suit. It's a color pattern, not a breed."],
  ["why do cats land on their feet | how do cats always land on their feet", "Cats have a **righting reflex**: as they fall, they twist the front half of their body and then the back half to turn feet-down. Their flexible spine and lack of a fixed collarbone help. Even so, falls can still hurt cats, so keep windows and balconies safe."],
  ["why do cats' eyes glow in the dark | why do animal eyes shine at night", "Many animals, including cats, dogs and deer, have a shiny layer at the back of their eyes called the **tapetum lucidum**. It reflects light back through the eye to help them see in dim light, which makes their eyes glow when light hits them."],
  ["can cats see in the dark | can cats see in complete darkness", "Cats can't see in total darkness, but they see much better than people in dim light, needing only about a sixth of the light we do. Their big pupils and reflective eye layer help them hunt at dawn and dusk."],
  ["why do cats have whiskers | what are cat whiskers for", "**Whiskers** are super-sensitive hairs that help cats feel the air and nearby objects, even in the dark. They help cats judge if they can fit through a gap and sense movement around them. You should never trim a cat's whiskers."],
  ["why do cats like boxes | why do cats sit in boxes", "Cats love boxes because small, enclosed spaces make them feel safe and hidden, which is great for napping and pretend ambushes. Boxes are also cozy and warm, and cats like to stay warm."],
  ["why do cats bring you dead animals | why does my cat bring me mice", "When cats bring you a mouse or bird, they're following their hunting instincts and bringing their catch to a safe home base. Some people think they might be sharing or teaching you. Keeping cats indoors helps protect wild birds."],
  ["why do cats hiss | what does it mean when a cat hisses", "A hiss is a cat's way of saying \"back off, I'm scared or upset.\" It's a warning, not usually an attack. Give the cat space and time to calm down."],
  ["why do cats sleep so much | how many hours do cats sleep", "Cats often sleep 12 to 16 hours a day. Their wild relatives save energy between hunts, and house cats keep that habit. Cats are most active around dawn and dusk."],
  ["why do cats scratch furniture | why do cats scratch things", "Cats scratch to keep their claws healthy, stretch their muscles and leave their scent from glands in their paws. Giving them a sturdy scratching post helps protect your couch!"],
  ["why do cats hate water | do cats like water", "Most house cats dislike getting wet because soggy fur is heavy, cold and takes a long time to dry. Some breeds, like Bengals and Turkish Vans, actually enjoy water. Big cats like tigers and jaguars love to swim!"],
  ["how long do cats live | what is the lifespan of a cat", "House cats commonly live about 12 to 18 years, and many indoor cats live into their 20s. Good food, vet visits and staying indoors help cats live longer."],
  ["how long do dogs live | what is the lifespan of a dog", "Most dogs live around 10 to 13 years, but it depends a lot on size. Small dogs often live longer, sometimes 15 years or more, while giant breeds may live 8 to 10 years."],
  ["what are dog years | how do you calculate dog years | is one dog year seven human years", "The \"multiply by seven\" rule is just a rough guess. Dogs grow up very fast in their first year or two, then age more slowly, and big dogs age faster than small ones. A 1-year-old dog is already like a teenager!"],
  ["why do dogs pant | why do dogs breathe fast with their tongue out", "Dogs can't sweat much through their skin, so they cool off by **panting**. Breathing fast lets water evaporate from their tongue and mouth, carrying heat away. Always give dogs shade and water on hot days."],
  ["why do dogs bark | what are dogs saying when they bark", "Dogs bark to communicate: to alert you, warn strangers, ask for attention, or show they're excited, bored or scared. Different barks sound different, and you can learn your dog's \"language\" over time."],
  ["why do dogs eat grass | is it bad if my dog eats grass", "Many dogs eat grass now and then, and scientists aren't completely sure why. They might like the taste or texture, or be bored. It's usually harmless, but avoid grass with chemicals and ask a vet if it happens a lot."],
  ["why do dogs tilt their heads | why does my dog tilt its head when i talk", "Dogs may tilt their heads to hear better, to see your face around their muzzle, or because they're trying to understand words they know. It also gets them lots of attention, which they love!"],
  ["why do dogs have wet noses | why is a dog's nose wet", "A dog's wet nose helps it catch scent particles, giving it a better sense of smell. Licking also helps keep the nose moist. A dry nose doesn't always mean a dog is sick."],
  ["why do dogs lick you | why does my dog lick me", "Dogs lick to show love, to say hello, to get attention or because you taste salty. Puppies lick their mothers, so it's a natural friendly behavior."],
  ["why do dogs chase their tails | why does my dog chase its tail", "Puppies often chase their tails just for fun while discovering their bodies. Older dogs might do it when bored or to get attention. If a dog does it all the time, it's a good idea to see a vet."],
  ["why do dogs dig | why does my dog dig holes", "Dogs dig because their wild ancestors dug dens, buried food and made cool spots to lie in. Some breeds, like terriers and dachshunds, were bred to dig. Boredom can also lead to digging."],
  ["why do dogs roll in smelly things | why does my dog roll in stinky stuff", "Dogs may roll in smelly things to cover their own scent, like wild wolves might do when hunting, or to bring the interesting smell back to their pack. To us it's gross, but to dogs it's like a great perfume!"],
  ["why do dogs howl at sirens | why do dogs howl", "Howling is a way dogs and wolves communicate over long distances. A siren may sound a little like another dog howling, so some dogs howl back to answer."],
  ["can dogs smell fear | how good is a dog's nose", "A dog's nose is amazing: it has hundreds of millions of scent sensors, many times more than ours. Dogs can pick up changes in our sweat and body chemistry, so they may notice when we're stressed or scared."],
  ["how do bees make honey | how is honey made", "Bees sip nectar from flowers and store it in a special honey stomach. Back at the hive, they pass it mouth-to-mouth, adding enzymes, then spread it into wax cells and fan it with their wings to dry it until it's thick honey. Finally, they cap the cells with wax."],
  ["why do bees dance | what is the waggle dance", "Honeybees do a **waggle dance** to tell other bees where to find flowers. The angle of the dance shows the direction compared to the sun, and how long they waggle shows how far away the food is."],
  ["do sharks sleep | how do sharks sleep", "Sharks don't sleep like we do, but they do have rest periods. Some sharks must keep swimming to push water over their gills, while others, like nurse sharks, can rest on the sea floor and pump water over their gills."],
  ["how do dolphins sleep | do dolphins sleep with one eye open", "Dolphins sleep with **half their brain at a time** while the other half stays alert. This lets them keep breathing, watch for danger and stay with their group. They often keep one eye open while resting."],
  ["how do whales breathe | do whales breathe air", "Whales are mammals, so they breathe air through blowholes on top of their heads. When a whale surfaces, it blows out warm, wet air in a big spout. Some whales can hold their breath for over an hour."],
  ["why do whales sing | what are whale songs", "Whales make songs and calls to communicate across long distances underwater. Male humpback whales sing long, complex songs, probably to attract mates. Sound travels very well in water, so some calls can be heard far away."],
  ["how do birds sleep | do birds sleep in nests", "Most birds don't sleep in nests; nests are mainly for eggs and chicks. Birds usually sleep perched on branches, where special tendons lock their toes tight so they don't fall. Some can even sleep with half their brain awake."],
  ["why don't birds get shocked on power lines | why can birds sit on wires", "Birds don't get shocked because electricity only flows when there's a path to something with a different voltage, like the ground or another wire. A bird touching just one wire isn't making that path, so the electricity stays in the wire."],
  ["why do geese fly in a v | why do birds fly in a v shape", "Flying in a **V shape** saves energy. Each bird gets a little lift from the air swirling off the wingtips of the bird ahead. The birds take turns at the front, where it's hardest."],
  ["how do birds know where to migrate | how do birds find their way when migrating", "Migrating birds use several tools: the position of the sun and stars, Earth's magnetic field, landmarks like coastlines and mountains, and even smells. Young birds of some species know the direction by instinct on their very first trip."],
  ["how do birds build nests | why do birds build nests", "Birds build nests to keep their eggs and chicks safe and warm. They use twigs, grass, mud, feathers, spider silk and even bits of string, and each species has its own style. Some birds just lay eggs on bare ground or cliffs!"],
  ["can birds smell | do birds have a good sense of smell", "Yes, many birds can smell! Vultures can sniff out food from far away, and seabirds like albatrosses follow smells across the ocean. Kiwis use smell to find worms in the ground."],
  ["do birds have teeth | why don't birds have teeth", "Modern birds don't have teeth; they use their beaks to grab food and a muscular stomach part called a **gizzard** to grind it up. Some ancient birds did have teeth. Baby birds have an \"egg tooth\" to break out of the shell, but it falls off."],
  ["why do roosters crow | why do roosters crow in the morning", "**Roosters** crow to announce their territory and tell other roosters \"this is my place.\" They have an internal body clock that makes them crow most around dawn, but they can crow any time of day."],
  ["why do moths fly toward light | why are bugs attracted to light", "Scientists think artificial lights confuse flying insects. Instead of flying straight toward a light, insects tilt their backs toward it, as if it were the sky, and end up circling around it. That's why moths flutter around porch lights."],
  ["how do flies walk on ceilings | how do insects walk upside down", "Flies have tiny claws plus sticky, hairy pads on their feet that grip almost any surface. That lets them walk upside down on ceilings and even up glass windows."],
  ["do spiders sleep | do insects sleep", "Many insects and spiders have rest periods that are a lot like sleep. Bees, fruit flies and cockroaches slow down and become less responsive, and jumping spiders even seem to have dream-like twitching during their rest."],
  ["how do insects breathe | do bugs have lungs", "Insects don't have lungs. Air enters through tiny holes along their sides called **spiracles** and travels through little tubes straight to their body's cells."],
  ["how do snakes smell with their tongues | why do snakes flick their tongues", "Snakes flick their forked tongues to collect scent particles from the air and ground. The tongue brings them to a special sense organ on the roof of the mouth, called **Jacobson's organ**. The fork helps them tell which direction a smell comes from."],
  ["why do snakes shed their skin | how often do snakes shed", "Snakes shed their skin because the outer layer doesn't grow with them. They rub against rough things and wriggle out, often in one long piece, even including the eye covers! Young, growing snakes shed more often than adults."],
  ["do snakes have ears | can snakes hear", "Snakes don't have outer ears or eardrums, but they do have inner ears. They feel vibrations through the ground with their jawbones and can hear some low sounds in the air."],
  ["why do lizards drop their tails | can lizards regrow their tails", "Many lizards can drop their tails when grabbed, and the tail keeps wiggling to distract the predator while the lizard escapes. Most can regrow a new tail, though it's usually shorter and made of cartilage instead of bone."],
  ["how do fish swim | how do fish move", "Fish swim by bending their bodies and sweeping their tails from side to side, pushing water backward. Fins help them steer, balance and stop. Many fish also have a gas-filled **swim bladder** that helps them float at the right depth."],
  ["why do fish swim in schools | what is a school of fish", "Swimming in a **school** helps fish stay safe, since it's hard for a predator to pick one target from a swirling crowd. Schools also help fish find food and save energy by swimming in each other's wake."],
  ["how do animals communicate | do animals talk to each other", "Animals communicate with sounds, body language, colors, smells, touch and even electrical signals. Bees dance, birds sing, wolves howl, ants leave scent trails and fireflies flash lights. Each species has its own kind of \"language.\""],
  ["do animals have feelings | can animals feel happy or sad", "Scientists have found that many animals, especially mammals and birds, show signs of feelings like fear, joy, stress and attachment. Elephants, dogs and many others seem to grieve, play and bond. That's one reason it's important to treat animals kindly."],
  ["why do animals play | do animals play for fun", "Young animals play to practice skills they'll need later, like hunting, fighting and escaping, and to build friendships. Many adult animals, like dogs, dolphins, ravens and otters, also seem to play just for fun."],
  ["what is echolocation | which animals use echolocation", "**Echolocation** is finding things by making sounds and listening for the echoes. Bats, dolphins, toothed whales and some birds use it to find food and get around in the dark or in murky water. Some blind people have learned to use clicks in a similar way!"],
  ["how do animals stay warm in the arctic | how do polar animals survive the cold", "Polar animals have thick fur or feathers, layers of fat called **blubber**, and small ears and tails that lose less heat. Many huddle together, and some, like walruses and seals, can reduce blood flow to their skin to save warmth."],
  ["how do desert animals survive | how do animals live in the desert", "Desert animals often stay underground or in shade during the day and come out at night. Many get water from their food, have big ears to release heat, and have bodies that save water. Camels, fennec foxes and kangaroo rats are great examples."],
  ["what animals live in the rainforest | rainforest animals", "Rainforests are home to more kinds of animals than any other land habitat! Jaguars, toucans, sloths, poison dart frogs, orangutans, gorillas, tapirs, capybaras, leafcutter ants and morpho butterflies all live in rainforests."],
  ["what animals live in the arctic | arctic animals", "Arctic animals include polar bears, arctic foxes, walruses, seals, narwhals, beluga whales, reindeer, musk oxen, arctic hares, snowy owls and lemmings. Penguins don't live there; they're in the Southern Hemisphere."],
  ["what animals live in antarctica | antarctic animals", "Antarctica's animals mostly live in or near the ocean: penguins, seals like the leopard seal and Weddell seal, whales, seabirds like albatrosses, and tons of krill. Very few animals live on the frozen land itself, mostly tiny insects and mites."],
  ["what animals live in the desert | desert animals", "Desert animals include camels, fennec foxes, meerkats, scorpions, rattlesnakes, roadrunners, Gila monsters, thorny devils, jerboas, kangaroo rats and oryx. They all have special ways to survive heat and very little water."],
  ["what animals only live in australia | unique australian animals", "Australia has animals found nowhere else in the wild, like kangaroos, koalas, wombats, platypuses, echidnas, Tasmanian devils, quokkas, kookaburras, emus and lyrebirds. Its long isolation from other continents let these unique creatures evolve."],
  ["what animals live on a farm | farm animals list", "Common farm animals include cows, pigs, sheep, goats, chickens, ducks, turkeys, geese, horses, donkeys and rabbits. Some farms also raise llamas, alpacas, bees or fish. Farm animals give us food, wool, and help with work."],
  ["what is the best pet for a kid | what is a good first pet", "Good first pets can include fish, guinea pigs, rabbits, hamsters, cats or dogs, depending on your family's time and space. Every pet needs daily care, so talk with a grown-up about who will feed, clean and play with it."],
  ["what is a vertebrate | what animals are vertebrates", "A **vertebrate** is an animal with a backbone. The five main groups are mammals, birds, reptiles, amphibians and fish. Humans, sharks, frogs, eagles and snakes are all vertebrates."],
  ["what is an invertebrate | what animals are invertebrates", "An **invertebrate** is an animal without a backbone. Insects, spiders, worms, snails, crabs, octopuses, jellyfish and sea stars are all invertebrates. About 95% or more of all animal species are invertebrates!"],
  ["what are the five groups of vertebrates | what are the animal groups | how are animals classified", "Vertebrates are usually sorted into five main groups: **mammals** (fur, milk), **birds** (feathers), **reptiles** (dry scales), **amphibians** (moist skin, live in water and on land) and **fish** (gills and fins). Invertebrates are split into many more groups, like insects, arachnids, crustaceans and mollusks."],
  ["what is the difference between mammals and reptiles | mammals vs reptiles", "**Mammals** have hair or fur, are warm-blooded, and feed their babies milk; most give birth to live young. **Reptiles** have dry, scaly skin, are cold-blooded and most lay eggs. Dogs and whales are mammals, while snakes and lizards are reptiles."],
  ["what is the difference between warm blooded and cold blooded | warm blooded vs cold blooded animals", "**Warm-blooded** animals, like mammals and birds, make their own body heat and keep a steady temperature. **Cold-blooded** animals, like reptiles, amphibians and most fish, depend on their surroundings, so they bask in the sun to warm up and seek shade to cool off."],
  ["are birds warm blooded | are fish cold blooded | are snakes cold blooded", "Birds are warm-blooded, just like mammals, and keep a steady body temperature. Snakes, lizards, frogs and most fish are cold-blooded, so their body temperature changes with their surroundings. A few fish, like tuna, can keep parts of their bodies warm."],
  ["what is a bird | what makes a bird a bird", "**Birds** are warm-blooded vertebrates with feathers, beaks and wings, and they lay hard-shelled eggs. Feathers are what make a bird a bird; no other living animals have them. Not all birds fly, like penguins, ostriches and kiwis."],
  ["what is a fish | what makes a fish a fish", "**Fish** are vertebrates that live in water, breathe with gills and usually have fins and scales. Most are cold-blooded. There are more kinds of fish than all other vertebrates combined!"],
  ["what is an insect | what makes an insect an insect", "An **insect** has six legs, three body parts (head, thorax and abdomen), and usually antennae and wings. Insects are the largest group of animals on Earth. Beetles, ants, flies, butterflies and bees are all insects."],
  ["what is the difference between an insect and an arachnid | insects vs arachnids | what is an arachnid", "**Insects** have six legs, three body parts and usually antennae and wings. **Arachnids**, like spiders, scorpions, ticks and mites, have eight legs, two body parts and no antennae or wings."],
  ["what is an arthropod | what are arthropods", "**Arthropods** are invertebrates with a hard outer skeleton, a body in segments and jointed legs. Insects, spiders, crabs, lobsters, centipedes and millipedes are all arthropods. They're the biggest group of animals on Earth."],
  ["what is an exoskeleton | why do bugs have exoskeletons", "An **exoskeleton** is a hard skeleton on the outside of the body, like armor. Insects, spiders and crabs have one. Since it can't grow, they must molt, shedding the old one and growing a new, bigger one."],
  ["what is a crustacean | examples of crustaceans", "**Crustaceans** are arthropods with hard shells and two pairs of antennae. Most live in water and breathe with gills. Crabs, lobsters, shrimp, krill, barnacles and even pill bugs are crustaceans."],
  ["what is a mollusk | examples of mollusks", "**Mollusks** are soft-bodied invertebrates, and many have shells. Snails, slugs, clams, oysters and mussels are mollusks, and so are octopuses, squid and cuttlefish, which are some of the smartest invertebrates."],
  ["what is a cephalopod | what are cephalopods", "**Cephalopods** are mollusks with arms or tentacles attached to their heads. Octopuses, squid, cuttlefish and nautiluses are cephalopods. Many can change color and squirt ink, and they're very smart."],
  ["what is an echinoderm | what are echinoderms", "**Echinoderms** are spiny-skinned sea animals with bodies often arranged in five parts. Starfish, sea urchins, sand dollars and sea cucumbers are echinoderms. They move using tiny tube feet."],
  ["what is a cnidarian | what animals sting with tentacles", "**Cnidarians** are water animals with stinging cells on their tentacles. Jellyfish, corals and sea anemones are cnidarians. Most have a simple body with one opening used as both mouth and exit."],
  ["what is a primate | what animals are primates", "**Primates** are mammals with grasping hands, forward-facing eyes and big brains. Lemurs, monkeys, apes and humans are all primates. Most live in trees in warm parts of the world."],
  ["what is the difference between monkeys and apes | is a gorilla a monkey", "**Apes**, like gorillas, chimpanzees, orangutans, bonobos and gibbons, have no tails and are usually bigger with larger brains. Most **monkeys** have tails. So a gorilla is an ape, not a monkey!"],
  ["what is a rodent | what animals are rodents", "**Rodents** are mammals with big front teeth that never stop growing, so they gnaw to keep them short. Mice, rats, squirrels, hamsters, beavers, porcupines and capybaras are rodents. They're the biggest group of mammals."],
  ["is a rabbit a rodent | are rabbits rodents", "No, **rabbits** aren't rodents; they're **lagomorphs**, along with hares and pikas. Like rodents, their teeth keep growing, but rabbits have an extra pair of small front teeth behind the top ones."],
  ["what is a marsupial | what animals have pouches | why do kangaroos have pouches", "**Marsupials** are mammals whose babies are born tiny and keep growing inside the mother's pouch. Kangaroos, koalas, wombats, opossums and Tasmanian devils are marsupials. Most live in Australia and nearby, but opossums live in the Americas."],
  ["what is a monotreme | which mammals lay eggs | do any mammals lay eggs", "**Monotremes** are mammals that lay eggs! There are only a few kinds alive: the platypus and four species of echidna. After the eggs hatch, the babies drink milk like other mammals."],
  ["what is a carnivore | what animals are carnivores", "A **carnivore** is an animal that mainly eats meat. Lions, sharks, eagles, wolves, snakes and orcas are carnivores. They often have sharp teeth or claws for catching prey."],
  ["what is a herbivore | what animals are herbivores", "A **herbivore** is an animal that eats plants. Cows, deer, elephants, giraffes, rabbits and tortoises are herbivores. Many have flat teeth for grinding plants and long guts to digest them."],
  ["what is an omnivore | what animals are omnivores | are humans omnivores", "An **omnivore** eats both plants and animals. Bears, raccoons, pigs, chickens, many monkeys and most humans are omnivores. Being able to eat many foods helps them survive in lots of places."],
  ["what is an insectivore | what animals eat insects", "An **insectivore** mainly eats insects and other bugs. Anteaters, aardvarks, many bats, shrews, frogs, chameleons and lots of birds are insectivores. They help keep bug numbers in balance."],
  ["what is a scavenger | what animals are scavengers", "A **scavenger** eats animals that are already dead. Vultures, hyenas, crabs, crows and some beetles scavenge. Scavengers help clean up nature and recycle nutrients."],
  ["what is a decomposer | what are decomposers", "**Decomposers** break down dead plants and animals into nutrients that go back into the soil. Fungi, bacteria and some worms and insects are decomposers. Without them, the world would be piled high with dead stuff!"],
  ["what is a food chain | food chain example", "A **food chain** shows who eats whom. For example: grass is eaten by a grasshopper, which is eaten by a frog, which is eaten by a snake, which is eaten by a hawk. Energy from the sun passes along the chain from plants to animals."],
  ["food web explained | what is the difference between a food chain and a food web", "A **food web** is many food chains connected together, since most animals eat, and are eaten by, more than one thing. It shows how everything in an ecosystem is linked, so a change to one species can affect many others."],
  ["what are producers and consumers | what is a producer in a food chain", "**Producers**, like plants and algae, make their own food from sunlight. **Consumers** are animals that eat plants or other animals to get energy. Herbivores are primary consumers, and the animals that eat them are secondary consumers."],
  ["what is a predator | what is prey | predator and prey examples", "A **predator** hunts and eats other animals, and **prey** are the animals that get eaten. A fox is a predator of rabbits, so rabbits are the fox's prey. Many animals are both, like a frog that eats flies but is eaten by snakes."],
  ["what is an apex predator | what is a top predator", "An **apex predator** is at the top of its food chain, with no natural predators as an adult. Orcas, lions, tigers, polar bears, saltwater crocodiles and great white sharks are apex predators. They help keep ecosystems balanced."],
  ["what is a keystone species | why are some animals so important to ecosystems", "A **keystone species** has a huge effect on its ecosystem compared to its numbers. For example, sea otters eat sea urchins, which keeps urchins from destroying kelp forests. Beavers, wolves and elephants are also keystone species."],
  ["ecosystem vs habitat | what is a habitat", "An **ecosystem** is a community of living things, like plants, animals and microbes, plus their surroundings, all working together. A **habitat** is the natural home where a particular animal lives, like a pond, desert or forest."],
  ["what is an adaptation | examples of animal adaptations", "An **adaptation** is a feature or behavior that helps an animal survive in its home. A polar bear's thick fur, a camel's ability to go without water, and a giraffe's long neck are adaptations."],
  ["how do animals evolve | how do new animal species form", "**Evolution** is how living things change over many generations. Animals with features that help them survive tend to have more babies, passing those features on. Over very long times, this can create new species. This idea is called **natural selection**."],
  ["what counts as a species | how many animal species are there", "A **species** is a group of living things that can have babies together that can also have babies. Scientists have named over 1.5 million animal species, and many millions more probably haven't been discovered yet, mostly insects."],
  ["what is taxonomy | what are kingdom phylum class order family genus species", "**Taxonomy** is how scientists organize living things into groups, from big to small: kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species. A memory trick is \"King Philip Came Over For Good Soup.\""],
  ["what is a scientific name | why do animals have latin names", "Every species has a two-part **scientific name**, like *Panthera leo* for the lion, made of its genus and species. Scientists everywhere use the same name, so there's no confusion between languages or nicknames."],
  ["what does nocturnal mean | what animals are nocturnal", "**Nocturnal** animals are active at night and sleep during the day. Owls, bats, raccoons, hedgehogs, opossums and many cats are nocturnal. They often have big eyes or great hearing and smell to find food in the dark."],
  ["what does diurnal mean | what does crepuscular mean", "**Diurnal** animals, like most birds, squirrels and humans, are active during the day. **Crepuscular** animals, like deer, rabbits and many house cats, are most active at dawn and dusk."],
  ["what animals hibernate | which animals sleep all winter", "Animals that hibernate include ground squirrels, groundhogs, hedgehogs, many bats, dormice and some turtles and frogs. Bears go into a deep winter rest that's very similar. During hibernation, heart rate and breathing slow way down to save energy."],
  ["do animals eat during hibernation | how do animals survive hibernation", "Most hibernating animals don't eat at all. They fatten up in autumn and live off their stored fat all winter. Some, like chipmunks, wake up now and then to snack from food they stored."],
  ["what is estivation | do animals sleep in summer", "**Estivation** is like hibernation but in hot, dry weather. Some snails, lungfish, desert frogs and tortoises go dormant underground or in mud to survive heat and drought, waking up when rain returns."],
  ["what is camouflage | how do animals use camouflage", "**Camouflage** is when an animal's colors or shape help it blend into its surroundings. It helps prey hide from predators and helps predators sneak up on prey. Stick insects, snowshoe hares, leaf-tailed geckos and flounders are camouflage experts."],
  ["what is mimicry in animals | why do some animals look like other animals", "**Mimicry** is when an animal copies the look of something else. Some harmless hoverflies look like stinging wasps, and some caterpillars have fake eye spots that look like a snake's eyes, so predators leave them alone."],
  ["what are warning colors | why are some animals brightly colored", "Bright colors like red, yellow and black often warn predators \"I'm poisonous or I taste bad!\" This is called **warning coloration**. Poison dart frogs, ladybugs, wasps and coral snakes use it."],
  ["what is complete metamorphosis | what is incomplete metamorphosis", "In **complete metamorphosis**, an insect goes through four stages: egg, larva, pupa and adult, like butterflies, beetles and bees. In **incomplete metamorphosis**, there are three stages: egg, nymph and adult, and the nymph looks like a small adult, like grasshoppers and dragonflies."],
  ["what is the difference between a cocoon and a chrysalis | chrysalis vs cocoon", "A **chrysalis** is the hard case a butterfly caterpillar forms when it changes into a butterfly. A **cocoon** is a silk wrapping that many moth caterpillars spin around themselves. Both protect the insect while it transforms."],
  ["what is a tadpole | how does a tadpole turn into a frog", "A **tadpole** is a baby frog or toad that lives in water and breathes with gills. Over weeks, it grows back legs, then front legs, its tail shrinks and it develops lungs. Then it hops onto land as a little frog!"],
  ["what is a larva | what is a pupa", "A **larva** is the young, worm-like stage of many insects, like a caterpillar or maggot, and it mostly eats and grows. A **pupa** is the resting stage where the larva's body is rebuilt into an adult."],
  ["what is symbiosis | what is mutualism", "**Symbiosis** is when two different species live closely together. In **mutualism**, both benefit, like clownfish and anemones or bees and flowers. In parasitism, one benefits and the other is harmed, like a tick on a dog."],
  ["what is a parasite | examples of parasites", "A **parasite** lives on or in another living thing, called the host, and takes food from it, often harming it. Fleas, ticks, lice and tapeworms are parasites. Cuckoos are even called brood parasites."],
  ["why do some animals live in groups | what is a herd", "Living in groups helps animals spot danger, defend themselves, find food and raise young. Herds of zebras, packs of wolves, flocks of birds, schools of fish and pods of dolphins all work together this way."],
  ["what is the difference between horns and antlers | horns vs antlers", "**Antlers** are made of bone, branch out, and fall off and regrow every year, like on deer and moose. **Horns** have a bone core covered in keratin, usually don't branch and stay for life, like on cows, goats and antelopes."],
  ["what are tusks | which animals have tusks", "**Tusks** are extra-long teeth that stick out of the mouth. Elephants, walruses, narwhals, warthogs and hippos have tusks. They use them for digging, fighting, lifting and showing off."],
  ["why do animals go extinct | what causes extinction", "Animals can go extinct when their habitats are destroyed, when they're overhunted or overfished, from pollution and climate change, or when new predators and diseases arrive. In the far past, natural disasters like giant asteroid impacts also caused extinctions."],
  ["what is a mass extinction | how many mass extinctions have there been", "A **mass extinction** is when a huge number of species die out in a fairly short time. Scientists count five big ones in Earth's history. The most famous wiped out the non-bird dinosaurs about 66 million years ago."],
  ["how can we protect endangered animals | how can i help animals", "You can help by protecting habitats, planting native flowers for pollinators, reducing waste and plastic, never buying products from endangered animals and supporting wildlife groups and good zoos. Learning and teaching others about animals helps too!"],
  ["what is conservation | what do conservationists do", "**Conservation** means protecting nature, including animals, plants and their habitats. Conservationists create protected parks, breed endangered animals, study wildlife and work to stop poaching and pollution."],
  ["what is poaching | why is poaching bad", "**Poaching** is illegally hunting or catching wild animals, often for things like ivory, horns, skins or pets. It has pushed animals like rhinos, elephants, tigers and pangolins toward extinction, so many countries fight hard to stop it."],
  ["animals that came back from near extinction | what animals were saved from extinction", "Some animals have bounced back thanks to protection, like the American bison, bald eagle, humpback whale, California condor and southern white rhino. These success stories show conservation can work!"],
  ["what was the tasmanian tiger | tell me about the thylacine", "The **thylacine**, or Tasmanian tiger, was a striped, dog-like marsupial predator from Australia and Tasmania. The last known one died in a zoo in 1936. It's a reminder of how important it is to protect animals before it's too late."],
  ["tell me about t rex | t rex facts | tyrannosaurus rex facts", "**Tyrannosaurus rex** lived about 68 to 66 million years ago in what's now western North America. It was up to about 12 meters (40 feet) long and had one of the strongest bites of any land animal ever. Its arms were short but very muscular."],
  ["why did t rex have small arms | what were t rex arms used for", "Scientists still debate why **T. rex** had such short arms. Its giant head and jaws did the hunting, so arms may have shrunk over time. The arms were still strong and might have helped hold prey or push up from the ground."],
  ["tell me about triceratops | triceratops facts", "**Triceratops** was a plant-eating dinosaur with three horns and a big bony frill on its head. It lived at the same time and place as T. rex, about 68 to 66 million years ago. Its name means \"three-horned face.\""],
  ["tell me about stegosaurus | stegosaurus facts | what were stegosaurus plates for", "**Stegosaurus** was a plant-eater with big plates along its back and four sharp spikes on its tail. The tail spikes, nicknamed the **thagomizer**, were for defense. It lived about 150 million years ago, so it was gone long before T. rex appeared."],
  ["tell me about velociraptor | velociraptor facts | how big was a velociraptor", "The real **Velociraptor** was about the size of a turkey, much smaller than in movies, and it had feathers! It lived about 75 million years ago in what's now Mongolia and had a big curved claw on each foot."],
  ["tell me about brachiosaurus | brachiosaurus facts", "**Brachiosaurus** was a giant long-necked plant-eater that lived about 150 million years ago. Its front legs were longer than its back legs, so it stood tall like a giraffe and could reach high treetops."],
  ["tell me about diplodocus | diplodocus facts", "**Diplodocus** was one of the longest dinosaurs, about 25 meters (80 feet) from head to tail. It had a long neck and a whip-like tail and ate plants. It lived about 150 million years ago."],
  ["tell me about ankylosaurus | ankylosaurus facts", "**Ankylosaurus** was like a living tank, covered in bony armor plates with a heavy club at the end of its tail. It was a plant-eater that lived at the end of the age of dinosaurs, around the same time as T. rex."],
  ["tell me about spinosaurus | spinosaurus facts", "**Spinosaurus** was a huge meat-eating dinosaur with a tall sail on its back and a long, crocodile-like snout. It lived in North Africa about 95 million years ago and spent a lot of time in and around water hunting fish."],
  ["tell me about parasaurolophus | parasaurolophus facts", "**Parasaurolophus** was a duck-billed dinosaur with a long, hollow tube-shaped crest on its head. Scientists think air moving through the crest could make deep, trumpet-like sounds to communicate."],
  ["tell me about iguanodon | iguanodon facts", "**Iguanodon** was a plant-eating dinosaur with spiky thumbs and one of the first dinosaurs ever named, back in 1825. At first, scientists wrongly put its thumb spike on its nose!"],
  ["tell me about allosaurus | allosaurus facts", "**Allosaurus** was a large meat-eating dinosaur that lived about 150 million years ago, alongside Stegosaurus. It was a top predator of its time, with sharp, serrated teeth."],
  ["tell me about pachycephalosaurus | which dinosaur had a thick head", "**Pachycephalosaurus** had a super-thick, dome-shaped skull up to about 25 cm (10 inches) thick. Its name means \"thick-headed lizard.\" Scientists debate whether it head-butted rivals or used its dome mainly for display."],
  ["tell me about archaeopteryx | archaeopteryx facts | what was the first bird", "**Archaeopteryx** lived about 150 million years ago and is one of the earliest known birds. It had feathers and wings like a bird, but also teeth, clawed fingers and a long bony tail like a dinosaur, showing how birds came from dinosaurs."],
  ["tell me about pteranodon | pteranodon facts", "**Pteranodon** was a flying reptile, not a dinosaur, with a wingspan around 6 meters (20 feet) and a long crest on its head. It had no teeth and probably flew over the sea catching fish."],
  ["tell me about quetzalcoatlus | what was the biggest flying animal ever", "**Quetzalcoatlus** was a giant pterosaur and one of the largest flying animals ever, with a wingspan of around 10 to 11 meters (about 35 feet). Standing on the ground, it was about as tall as a giraffe!"],
  ["tell me about mosasaurus | mosasaurus facts", "**Mosasaurus** was a huge marine reptile, not a dinosaur, that ruled the seas at the end of the dinosaur age. It could grow over 12 meters (40 feet) long and was related to modern lizards and snakes."],
  ["tell me about plesiosaurs | plesiosaur facts | is the loch ness monster a plesiosaur", "**Plesiosaurs** were marine reptiles with long necks, small heads and four big flippers. They died out about 66 million years ago. Some people imagine the Loch Ness Monster as a plesiosaur, but there's no evidence any survived."],
  ["tell me about ichthyosaurs | ichthyosaur facts", "**Ichthyosaurs** were marine reptiles that looked a lot like dolphins, even though they weren't related. Some had enormous eyes for seeing in deep water. They gave birth to live babies in the sea."],
  ["tell me about dimetrodon | is dimetrodon a dinosaur", "No! **Dimetrodon** had a big sail on its back and looks like a dinosaur, but it lived about 290 million years ago, long before dinosaurs. It's actually more closely related to mammals than to dinosaurs."],
  ["tell me about megalodon | megalodon facts | is megalodon still alive", "**Megalodon** was a giant prehistoric shark that may have grown around 15 meters (50 feet) or more, with teeth bigger than your hand. It died out about 3.6 million years ago, so it isn't alive today. Scientists mostly know it from its fossil teeth."],
  ["tell me about woolly mammoths | woolly mammoth facts | when did mammoths go extinct", "**Woolly mammoths** were relatives of elephants with long shaggy hair and curved tusks, built for the Ice Age. Most died out about 10,000 years ago, but a small group lived on Wrangel Island until about 4,000 years ago, while the Egyptian pyramids were being built!"],
  ["what is the difference between a mammoth and a mastodon | tell me about mastodons", "**Mastodons** and **mammoths** were both elephant relatives. Mastodons were shorter and stockier with cone-shaped teeth for browsing leaves and twigs. Mammoths had flatter, ridged teeth for grinding grass."],
  ["tell me about saber toothed cats | saber tooth tiger facts | what was smilodon", "**Smilodon**, the saber-toothed cat, had huge curved fangs up to about 18 cm (7 inches) long. It wasn't actually a tiger, but a different branch of the cat family. It died out about 10,000 years ago, and many fossils were found in the La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles."],
  ["tell me about woolly rhinos | woolly rhinoceros facts", "The **woolly rhinoceros** was an Ice Age rhino covered in thick fur, with a long front horn. Early humans painted woolly rhinos on cave walls in Europe."],
  ["tell me about giant ground sloths | what was megatherium", "**Giant ground sloths** like *Megatherium* were as big as elephants and lived in the Americas until roughly 10,000 years ago. Unlike today's tree sloths, they walked on the ground and could stand up on their back legs to eat leaves."],
  ["tell me about dire wolves | were dire wolves real", "Yes, **dire wolves** were real! They lived in the Americas until about 10,000 years ago and were a bit bigger and heavier than today's gray wolves, with strong bites."],
  ["tell me about glyptodons | what was a glyptodon", "**Glyptodons** were giant prehistoric relatives of armadillos, about the size of a small car! They had a huge dome-shaped shell and lived in South America until about 10,000 years ago."],
  ["tell me about trilobites | trilobite facts", "**Trilobites** were sea creatures with hard, segmented shells that lived for nearly 300 million years, starting over 500 million years ago. They're related to insects, spiders and crabs and are among the most common fossils."],
  ["tell me about ammonites | ammonite facts", "**Ammonites** were sea creatures with spiral shells related to today's octopuses and squid. They died out with the dinosaurs about 66 million years ago, and their coiled fossils are found all over the world."],
  ["tell me about dunkleosteus | dunkleosteus facts", "**Dunkleosteus** was a giant armored fish that lived about 360 million years ago. Instead of teeth, it had sharp, bony blades in its jaws that worked like scissors."],
  ["tell me about terror birds | what were terror birds", "**Terror birds** were huge flightless meat-eating birds that lived mostly in South America after the dinosaurs died out. Some stood around 2 to 3 meters (7 to 10 feet) tall with big hooked beaks."],
  ["tell me about titanoboa | what was the biggest snake ever", "**Titanoboa** was a giant snake that lived about 60 million years ago in what's now Colombia. It was around 13 meters (about 42 feet) long and may have weighed more than a ton, much larger than any snake alive today."],
  ["tell me about argentinosaurus | argentinosaurus facts", "**Argentinosaurus** was a gigantic long-necked plant-eater from South America and one of the biggest land animals that ever lived. Scientists estimate it was around 30 to 35 meters long and weighed as much as several big elephants put together, but only a few bones have been found."],
  ["tell me about giganotosaurus | giganotosaurus facts", "**Giganotosaurus** was a huge meat-eating dinosaur from South America, about as long as T. rex or a bit longer. It had a lighter build and narrower teeth made for slicing. It lived millions of years before T. rex."],
  ["tell me about compsognathus | what was the smallest dinosaur", "**Compsognathus** was one of the smallest dinosaurs known, about the size of a chicken. It was a fast little hunter of lizards and insects. Hummingbirds are technically dinosaurs too, which makes them the smallest ones of all!"],
  ["tell me about dilophosaurus | did dilophosaurus spit venom", "**Dilophosaurus** was a meat-eating dinosaur with two thin crests on its head. In real life, there's no evidence that it spat venom or had a neck frill; those were made up for a movie. It was also much bigger than shown on screen."],
  ["tell me about therizinosaurus | which dinosaur had the longest claws", "**Therizinosaurus** had some of the longest claws of any animal ever, up to about 1 meter (3 feet) long! Even though it looked scary, it probably ate mostly plants and used its claws to pull down branches."],
  ["tell me about microraptor | which dinosaur had four wings", "**Microraptor** was a small, crow-sized dinosaur with feathered wings on both its arms and legs, like four wings! Fossils show its feathers were shiny black, a bit like a crow's."],
  ["tell me about maiasaura | maiasaura facts", "**Maiasaura** means \"good mother lizard.\" Fossils of its nests, eggs and babies show that these duck-billed dinosaurs cared for their young after they hatched."],
  ["what are the triassic jurassic and cretaceous periods | what is the mesozoic era", "The age of dinosaurs is called the **Mesozoic Era** and has three periods: the **Triassic** (about 252 to 201 million years ago), when dinosaurs first appeared; the **Jurassic** (about 201 to 145 million years ago), with giant long-necks; and the **Cretaceous** (about 145 to 66 million years ago), home of T. rex and Triceratops."],
  ["what does the word dinosaur mean | who named the dinosaurs", "**Dinosaur** means \"terrible lizard\" in Greek. The name was created by British scientist Richard Owen in 1842. Dinosaurs weren't actually lizards, though!"],
  ["how are fossils made | how do fossils form", "Most fossils form when an animal or plant is buried quickly in mud or sand. Over millions of years, layers pile up and minerals slowly replace the bones or shells, turning them to stone. Footprints, eggs and even poop can become fossils too!"],
  ["how do scientists know how old fossils are | how do we date fossils", "Scientists date fossils by figuring out the age of the rock layers around them. They measure tiny amounts of radioactive elements in certain rocks, which break down at a known rate like a natural clock. Deeper layers are usually older."],
  ["did dinosaurs have feathers | which dinosaurs had feathers", "Many dinosaurs had feathers! Fossils show feathers on Velociraptor, Microraptor and many other meat-eating dinosaurs, and even some plant-eaters had fuzzy filaments. Big adult T. rex probably had mostly scaly skin, though."],
  ["did dinosaurs lay eggs | how big were dinosaur eggs", "Yes, all dinosaurs that we know of laid eggs! Even giant long-necked dinosaurs had eggs only about the size of a soccer ball or smaller. Many dinosaurs made nests, and some sat on their eggs like birds."],
  ["how do scientists know what color dinosaurs were | what color were dinosaurs", "In some very well-preserved fossils, scientists can see tiny color structures called **melanosomes**. By comparing their shapes with those in modern birds, they've figured out the colors of a few dinosaurs, like the black Microraptor and a reddish-brown, striped-tail Sinosauropteryx."],
  ["what did dinosaurs eat | were most dinosaurs meat eaters", "Most dinosaurs were plant-eaters, munching on ferns, conifers, cycads and later flowering plants. Meat-eaters like T. rex hunted other dinosaurs, and some, like Spinosaurus, ate fish. A few may have eaten both plants and meat."],
  ["were dinosaurs warm blooded or cold blooded | were dinosaurs cold blooded", "Scientists think many dinosaurs were somewhere in between, or even warm-blooded like their bird relatives. Evidence from bone growth and chemistry suggests many grew fast and kept fairly warm bodies, unlike today's cold-blooded reptiles."],
  ["what is amber | can bugs be trapped in amber", "**Amber** is fossilized tree resin. Sometimes insects, spiders, feathers and even a dinosaur's feathered tail got stuck in sticky resin and were perfectly preserved for millions of years."],
  ["what is a coprolite | what is fossilized poop", "A **coprolite** is fossilized poop! Scientists study coprolites to learn what ancient animals ate. Some dinosaur coprolites contain bits of bone or plants."],
  ["could scientists bring back dinosaurs | is jurassic park possible", "Not really. DNA breaks down over time and doesn't last anywhere near 66 million years, so there's no dinosaur DNA to work with. Some scientists are researching ways to bring back traits of more recently extinct animals like mammoths, though."],
  ["what was the biggest egg ever | which bird lays the biggest egg", "Among living birds, the **ostrich** lays the biggest eggs, each about as heavy as two dozen chicken eggs. The extinct **elephant bird** of Madagascar laid even bigger eggs, with room for over 100 chicken eggs' worth inside!"],
  ["what is the highest flying bird | which bird flies the highest", "**Rüppell's vulture** holds the record for the highest flying bird seen, at about 11,300 meters (37,000 feet), as high as a jet plane. **Bar-headed geese** also fly over the Himalayas during migration."],
  ["what animal has the most legs | which animal has the most legs", "A millipede found deep underground in Australia has over 1,300 legs, the most of any known animal. Despite their name, most millipedes have far fewer than a thousand legs."],
  ["what is the heaviest insect | what is the largest beetle", "The **goliath beetle** and the **giant weta** are among the heaviest insects, weighing about as much as a small bird or mouse. The **titan beetle** and **Hercules beetle** are among the longest beetles."],
  ["what animal has the longest tongue | longest tongue for its size", "For its body size, the **chameleon** has one of the longest tongues, often longer than its body. Giant anteaters have tongues about 60 cm (2 feet) long, and a giraffe's tongue is around 45 to 50 cm (about 18 to 20 inches)."],
  ["what is the biggest lizard | largest lizard in the world", "The **Komodo dragon** is the biggest living lizard, reaching around 3 meters (10 feet) long and weighing 70 kg (150 pounds) or more. It lives on a few islands in Indonesia."],
  ["what is the biggest rodent in the world | largest rodent", "The **capybara** of South America is the largest living rodent. It can weigh 50 kg (110 pounds) or more, about as much as a big dog."],
  ["how many hearts does a squid have | do squid have blue blood", "Like octopuses, squid have **three hearts**: two pump blood through the gills and one pumps it to the rest of the body. Their blood is blue because it uses copper to carry oxygen."],
  ["what animals can regrow body parts | which animals can regenerate", "Lots of animals can regrow body parts! Axolotls can regrow legs and even parts of their hearts and brains, starfish can regrow arms, lizards can regrow tails, and flatworms can regrow a whole body from a small piece."],
  ["what animals change color | which animals can change color", "Chameleons, octopuses, cuttlefish and squid can change color quickly to communicate or hide. Arctic foxes, stoats and snowshoe hares change their coat color with the seasons. Flounders can also shift colors to match the sea floor."],
  ["what animals can see ultraviolet light | can animals see colors we can't", "Many animals can see ultraviolet light that's invisible to us, including bees, many birds, some fish and reindeer. Flowers often have UV patterns that guide bees to nectar, like secret landing strips!"],
  ["what animals use tools | do animals use tools", "Yes! Chimpanzees use sticks to fish for termites, sea otters crack shells with rocks, crows bend twigs into hooks, and some dolphins wear sponges on their noses to protect them while searching the sea floor."],
  ["what are the smartest animals | which animals are the most intelligent", "Some of the smartest animals include chimpanzees, bonobos, orangutans, dolphins, elephants, crows, ravens, parrots, octopuses and pigs. Many of them can solve problems, use tools and remember things for years."],
  ["what animals mate for life | do any animals stay together forever", "Some animals often stay with one partner for many years or life, including swans, albatrosses, bald eagles, beavers, gibbons, prairie voles and some wolves. These pairs often raise their young together."],
  ["which animals carry their babies on their backs | animals that carry their babies", "Lots of animals carry their babies! Opossums, koalas, scorpions, wolf spiders, sloths, anteaters and many monkeys carry young on their backs. Kangaroos use pouches, and male seahorses carry eggs in a pouch too."],
  ["what animals are immune to snake venom | which animals can survive snake bites", "Some animals are very resistant to snake venom, like mongooses, honey badgers, hedgehogs and opossums. Their bodies have special changes that stop much of the venom from working."],
  ["what is the difference between venomous and poisonous | venomous vs poisonous", "**Venomous** animals inject toxins by biting or stinging, like snakes, spiders and scorpions. **Poisonous** animals are harmful when you touch or eat them, like poison dart frogs and pufferfish. Simple trick: if it bites you and you get sick, it's venomous; if you bite it and get sick, it's poisonous!"],
  ["what is a baby owl called | what is a baby hedgehog called | what is a baby goat called", "A baby owl is an **owlet**, a baby swan is a **cygnet** and a baby goat is a **kid**. Other fun ones: a baby echidna is a **puggle**, a baby hedgehog is a **hoglet** and a baby eel is an **elver**."],
  ["what is a group of owls called | what is a group of flamingos called", "A group of owls is called a **parliament**, and a group of flamingos is called a **flamboyance**! Other fun ones include a **dazzle** of zebras, a **tower** of giraffes, a **bloat** of hippos and a **crash** of rhinos."]
);
